#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186396577 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6ed6eeb5-a646-5f47-b6d8-f1078f550376" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Factors Giving an Exact ODE"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "65cb7b3b-ca02-57e0-a31c-2311ebcd6860" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "ÍII only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396579} {:answer/id #uuid "21a7e756-add5-5d08-a0b6-865f25787d6d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396580} {:answer/id #uuid "0417384c-c1ac-529e-bf13-223231a54322" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396581} {:answer/id #uuid "4737d57e-814c-5ac7-acfb-48ce13b5a3e9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396582} {:answer/id #uuid "95ce12e4-12c0-5c3c-9bc0-e72604c4d3e6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396583}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "65cb7b3b-ca02-57e0-a31c-2311ebcd6860" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "ÍII only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396579} :answer-field/id #uuid "c0ee641f-c56c-5e39-8179-c1c054897293" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396578}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8e4d2f85-2640-5963-a9fc-d023ea35768b" :question/math-academy-id "q-103178" :question/problem "Consider the following differential equation:\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\text{d}x+x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)\\text{d}y=0\n$$\n\nGiven that the differential equation is exact, which of the following functions could be $P(x,y)?$\n\nI. $y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)-y$\nII. $xy\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)$\nIII. $y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)$" :question/worked-solution "A differential equation of the form\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\text{d}x+Q(x,y)\\text{d}y=0\n$$\n\nis exact if it passes the exactness test, i.e.,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we're given $Q(x,y)=x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy).$ \n\nFirst, let's differentiate $Q(x,y)$ with respect to $x$:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy))=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)-xy\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)\n$$\n\nNow, let's apply $\\frac{∂}{∂y}$ to each of the given options:\n\n- First, we check $P(x,y)=y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)-y.$ This gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂y}(y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)-y)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)-xy\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)-1\\ne \\frac{∂Q}{∂x}\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $P(x,y)=y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)-y$ does *not* give an exact equation.\n\n- Next, we check $P(x,y)=xy\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂y}(xy\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy))=x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)-{x}^{2}y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)\\ne \\frac{∂Q}{∂x}\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $P(x,y)=xy\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)$ does *not* give an exact equation.\n\n- Finally, we check $P(x,y)=y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂y}(y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy))=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)-xy\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $P(x,y)=y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)$  gives an exact equation.\n\nHence, the correct answer is ''III only''."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186396584 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "31eacf5a-75a5-5b38-8c24-4b1598ecf3a7" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Points of Discontinuity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "54ba84a1-3d25-5cd1-98bf-f731c2622e1d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=2$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396586} {:answer/id #uuid "da75cf17-b00a-5ed0-97c9-6265dae86079" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-1$ and $x=2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396587} {:answer/id #uuid "77df7b4b-c87e-5e29-808b-f38574caacd1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=1$ and $x=2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396588} {:answer/id #uuid "8c127ea3-d160-5664-896d-8df951530b12" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-2$ and $x=1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396589} {:answer/id #uuid "d02a6f82-debb-558b-a876-dc02aab50551" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-1$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396590}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "da75cf17-b00a-5ed0-97c9-6265dae86079" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-1$ and $x=2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396587} :answer-field/id #uuid "53daf543-087e-50bb-928d-d5b44cae5b1c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396585}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c6685bf3-2db8-58de-88bd-a80799c06e2f" :question/math-academy-id "q-104025" :question/problem "![](images/2d/2d342689f9e4a213ac5407ccc132868b51ba218afabda1a871e7ca7b02345a17.png)\n\nWhat are the points of discontinuity of the function plotted above?" :question/worked-solution "The graph breaks at $x=-1$ and $x=2,$ meaning that we can't draw the plot without taking our pencil off of the paper when passing through these points.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $x=-1$ and $x=2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186396591 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5130f54d-2a53-5373-81a6-355921ea7523" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Vector That Makes a Set Linearly Dependent"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e5f3b240-c702-580d-9cdf-43123253f773" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396593} {:answer/id #uuid "574dea89-ee9d-5556-a72b-c257c732699e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396594} {:answer/id #uuid "640a91d9-57f7-5e99-bc15-29e1456e8af0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396595} {:answer/id #uuid "2044901c-e971-534a-af35-fba5ec803324" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396596} {:answer/id #uuid "3bf8a5fb-65f3-58d7-a288-3f0a87f9681a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396597}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2044901c-e971-534a-af35-fba5ec803324" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396596} :answer-field/id #uuid "a01010bc-5e11-583a-98fc-7b20994c0fc5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396592}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "faa7ddb4-5b88-5e56-a53e-35fdb9d2ef93" :question/math-academy-id "q-105291" :question/problem "The set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ is linearly dependent. Which of the following vectors could be ${a}_{2}$ if\n${a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}9 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]?$" :question/worked-solution "We say that two vectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ are *linearly independent* if the only solution to the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{2}=0\n$$\n\nis ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}=0.$ Otherwise, the vectors are *linearly dependent*.\n\nA set containing *only two* vectors is linearly *dependent* if and only if the two vectors are parallel.\n\nFrom the given options, the correct answer is \n\n$$\n{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo see why, notice that \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]=\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}9 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]=\\frac{1}{3}{a}_{1}.\n$$\n\nThis way, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3}{a}_{1}-\\frac{1}{3}{a}_{1} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}{a}_{1}-{a}_{2} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn other words, the vectors ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{2}$ are parallel.\n\nTo see why this means that ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{2}$ are linearly dependent, we notice that the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}=0\n$$\n\nhas the solution ${x}_{1}=\\frac{1}{3}$ and ${x}_{2}=-1,$ which is different from ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}=0.$ Therefore, the set is linearly dependent."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186396598 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8d9b62ae-7545-5d65-bcf7-74ab0e8662ec" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Sets That Are Not Vector Spaces"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a58b77f-aa33-52d3-a1ab-4bbbe9ed2639" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "addition of polynomials is not associative" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396600} {:answer/id #uuid "cb6dcbd0-7e6a-5124-8dfb-30e54362007c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "addition and scalar multiplication of polynomials do not satisfy the distributive laws" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396601} {:answer/id #uuid "3afd6545-9a30-5d6b-bce2-014c2d72383f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "addition of polynomials in $Y$ is not closed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396602} {:answer/id #uuid "8ab25e26-7932-539a-b22c-b438766b2222" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "addition of polynomials is not commutative" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396603} {:answer/id #uuid "257d280c-e23a-51f0-8ab9-6c6a6d270300" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$1\\cdot p\\ne p$ for some $p\\in Y$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396604}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3afd6545-9a30-5d6b-bce2-014c2d72383f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "addition of polynomials in $Y$ is not closed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396602} :answer-field/id #uuid "8e07246a-b3d7-5b8e-9074-824239bfbe46" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396599}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4c206776-99db-55ba-ae48-7c0ec7d6d597" :question/math-academy-id "q-106929" :question/problem "Let $Y$ be the set of all polynomials in the variable $x\\in R$ with coefficients in $R$ of degree exactly $2.$ Complete the following sentence:\n\n*$Y$ is **not** a vector space (with respect to polynomials addition and multiplication of a polynomial by a real number) because $\\text{_________}\\,.$*" :question/worked-solution "The correct answer is as follows:\n\n*$Y$ is **not** a vector space (with respect to polynomials addition and multiplication of a polynomial by a real number) because **addition of polynomials in $Y$ is not closed.** *\n\nIndeed, let's consider, for example, the polynomials $p(x)={x}^{2}+x$ and $q(x)=-{x}^{2}.$ Then $p,q\\in Y,$ but \n\n$$\np(x)+q(x)={x}^{2}+x+(-{x}^{2})=x\\notin Y\n$$\n\n because $\\operatorname{deg}⁡(p+q)=1\\ne 2.$\n\nOn the other hand:\n\n- $1\\cdot p=p$ for all $p\\in Y.$ $\\,✓$\n\n- Addition and scalar multiplication of polynomials satisfy the distributive laws. $\\,✓$\n\n- Addition of polynomials is commutative. $\\,✓$\n\n- Addition of polynomials is associative. $\\,✓$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186396605 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8d9b62ae-7545-5d65-bcf7-74ab0e8662ec" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Sets That Are Not Vector Spaces"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b0fcd6c1-3e34-5869-bda3-f3935c249cfe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$1\\cdot f\\ne f$ for some $f\\in L$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396607} {:answer/id #uuid "9ae69b77-733b-5c58-bc2b-3800bcfe26dc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "multiplication of $f\\in L$ by a scalar is not closed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396608} {:answer/id #uuid "dac8d022-edf8-56b5-99c7-7f2b00e051d3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "addition of functions in $L$ is not associative" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396609} {:answer/id #uuid "6358779e-1da8-5238-9ef6-81ac25fc065f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "addition of functions in $L$ is not commutative" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396610} {:answer/id #uuid "85442eb9-7db8-592f-9879-ddfd77d97231" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "addition and scalar multiplication of functions in $L$ do not satisfy the distributive laws" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396611}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9ae69b77-733b-5c58-bc2b-3800bcfe26dc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "multiplication of $f\\in L$ by a scalar is not closed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396608} :answer-field/id #uuid "f09dfa2c-f035-539f-94db-4698ecc43f0a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396606}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "15beea94-47dc-575a-be81-e2d3b2547e2f" :question/math-academy-id "q-106930" :question/problem "Let $L$ be the set of all functions $f:R\\to R$ of the form $f(x)=ax+1,$ where $a\\in R.$ Complete the following sentence:\n\n*$L$ is **not** a vector space (with respect to function addition and scalar multiplication of a function by a real number) because $\\text{_________}\\,.$*" :question/worked-solution "The correct answer is as follows:\n\n*$L$ is **not** a vector space (with respect to function addition and scalar multiplication of a function by a real number) because **multiplication of $f\\in L$ by a scalar is not closed.** *\n\nFor example, if $\\alpha =2$ and $f(x)=x+1,$ then $\\alpha f(x)=2x+2.$ So, $\\alpha f(x)\\notin L$ because the constant term is not $1.$\n\nOn the other hand:\n\n- Addition of real valued functions is associative. $\\,✓$\n\n- Addition of real valued functions is commutative. $\\,✓$\n\n- $1\\cdot f=f$ for all $f\\in L.$ $\\,✓$\n\n- Addition and scalar multiplication of real valued functions satisfy the distributive laws.\n$\\,✓$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186396612 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8d9b62ae-7545-5d65-bcf7-74ab0e8662ec" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Sets That Are Not Vector Spaces"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "62b7fc67-e9bb-5a0f-8e80-9dcf56649314" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "addition of functions is not commutative" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396614} {:answer/id #uuid "7cd140b2-7871-5579-9e98-6ecf9f3f51ab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "addition of functions is not associative" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396615} {:answer/id #uuid "1e058a54-9658-5798-ba7c-a86d9840498e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "addition and scalar multiplication of functions do not satisfy the distributive laws" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396616} {:answer/id #uuid "d6932b0d-e307-5cc8-971a-38fac820cb2a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$1\\cdot f\\ne f$ for some $f\\in I$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396617} {:answer/id #uuid "4d629cc1-8c34-5761-b952-696843bcc069" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$I$ does not contain the zero element" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396618}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4d629cc1-8c34-5761-b952-696843bcc069" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$I$ does not contain the zero element" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396618} :answer-field/id #uuid "c7ef701a-1be0-572c-baca-f76e42023271" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396613}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "17d56701-363e-5629-ba43-f0ff54124002" :question/math-academy-id "q-106933" :question/problem "Let $I$ be the set of all functions $f:R\\to R$ of the form $f(x)={2}^{ax},$ where $a\\in R.$ Complete the following sentence:\n\n*$I$ is **not** a vector space (with respect to function addition and scalar multiplication of a function by a real number) because $\\text{_________}\\,.$*" :question/worked-solution "The correct answer is as follows:\n\n*$I$ is **not** a vector space (with respect to function addition and scalar multiplication of a function by a real number) because **$I$ does not contain the zero element.** *\n\nIndeed, $f(x)={2}^{ax}>0$ for all $x\\in R$ for any $a\\in R,$ hence the zero function $z(x)=0\\notin I.$\n\nOn the other hand:\n\n- Addition of real valued functions  is associative. $\\,✓$\n\n- $1\\cdot f=f$ for all $f\\in I.$ $\\,✓$\n\n- Addition and scalar multiplication of polynomials satisfy the distributive laws.\n$\\,✓$\n\n- Addition of real valued functions  is commutative. $\\,✓$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186396619 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bf19e2ee-7c42-58b0-9254-5a44f315288a" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Block Diagonal and Block Triangular Matrices"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c4860770-5064-5335-8f48-a983fc3e37dc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396621} {:answer/id #uuid "f1c0ad1a-1579-5a1d-9f6e-b8c9de4c4be8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396622} {:answer/id #uuid "776b96b0-8f5c-5b47-aa83-d1f82dd2dcf5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $B$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396623} {:answer/id #uuid "36e5fa30-29c5-544a-a26b-8772f9fe51ab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396624} {:answer/id #uuid "1eab1cd1-046e-5de3-9cd8-3170cc92f482" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A,$ $B,$ and $C$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396625}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1eab1cd1-046e-5de3-9cd8-3170cc92f482" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A,$ $B,$ and $C$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396625} :answer-field/id #uuid "b4cfca3f-c3d8-5194-b838-5df8371ad384" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396620}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c07f2a41-0b84-51be-b671-ed8827d6cdfb" :question/math-academy-id "q-109247" :question/problem "Which of the following matrices are block lower triangular?\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 3 & 2\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 9\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "A block lower triangular matrix can be partitioned so that it has only square blocks on the main diagonal, and the blocks above the main diagonal contain only zeros.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our matrices.\n\n- $A$ is a block lower triangular matrix. Indeed, we can partition it as follows:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 3 & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- $B$ is a block lower triangular matrix. Indeed, we can partition it as follows:\n\n$$\nB=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 9\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- $C$ is a block lower triangular matrix. Indeed, we can partition it as follows:\n\n$$\nC=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$A,$ $B,$ and $C.$\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186396626 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4f663b0b-9b5e-512c-92bb-0ea84d5baac5" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing the Limit of a Left Riemann Sum as a Definite Integral"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "34cc89b8-44ed-5197-913c-4b08fc997159" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f(x)={e}^{6x}$ and $b=6$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396628} {:answer/id #uuid "e2308dd8-f04b-55a3-a357-4d4562b92a56" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f(x)={e}^{x}$ and $b=6$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396629} {:answer/id #uuid "d9ab5d51-4c5d-5e5c-bd82-b3125ac54774" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f(x)={e}^{6x}$ and $b=1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396630} {:answer/id #uuid "68909286-13b5-56f8-91c1-b39ee0c858f5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f(x)={e}^{2x}$ and $b=3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396631} {:answer/id #uuid "e106c9d7-5e46-53e8-bd3f-34d8e8d97090" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f(x)={e}^{x}$ and $b=1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396632}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e2308dd8-f04b-55a3-a357-4d4562b92a56" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f(x)={e}^{x}$ and $b=6$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396629} :answer-field/id #uuid "224da74b-2945-552e-a5c2-10b9f76d854e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396627}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "c2081a2b-f662-517d-bd66-e0ddf60008b7" :question/math-academy-id "q-113918" :question/problem "Find the function $f(x)$ and the value of $b,$ given that ${\\int }_{0}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[{e}^{6i/n}\\cdot \\frac{6}{n}].$" :question/worked-solution "A definite integral can be defined as the limit of a left Riemann sum as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{i})\\cdot Δx\n$$\n\nwhere $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}$ and ${x}_{i}=a+iΔx.$\n\nNotice that we're given that the lower limit $a=0.$ \n\nComparing the given summation with the general expression above, we see that $Δx=\\frac{6}{n}.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\frac{6}{n}=\\frac{b-0}{n}\\,⟹\\,b=6.\n$$\n\nConsequently,  ${x}_{i}=\\frac{6i}{n}$ and $f({x}_{i})={e}^{{x}_{i}}.$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{6}{e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[{e}^{6i/n}\\cdot \\frac{6}{n}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $f(x)={e}^{x}$ and $b=6.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186396633 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2bc800b0-3901-5154-81ac-1ef853017320" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Partitions of the Real Line"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "706a81e0-fa08-57b0-b437-c2f1e80679af" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396635} {:answer/id #uuid "48b2153a-fd69-5239-a9c8-303b9cb48dd6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements give a partition of  $[-4,6]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396636} {:answer/id #uuid "cce4d51d-668c-5887-ab39-f0222a35d908" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396637} {:answer/id #uuid "4ed785a5-44c7-5d1c-ba4b-18ff8aed644c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396638} {:answer/id #uuid "43a1fd11-acfb-51ce-8e45-54f73c52e999" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396639}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "48b2153a-fd69-5239-a9c8-303b9cb48dd6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements give a partition of  $[-4,6]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396636} :answer-field/id #uuid "813feba2-b955-5d20-b059-a9fb82381546" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396634}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d62099c6-5c34-55aa-ad1f-5500db9aeb0d" :question/math-academy-id "q-122932" :question/problem "Which of the following are partitions of the real interval $[-4,6]?$\n\nI. $-5<-4<-2<0<4<6$\nII. ${-3,3,5,6}$\nIII. ${-4,4,5}$" :question/worked-solution "A partition $P$ of a closed interval $[a,b]$ is a finite sequence of real numbers taken from $[a,b]$ such that\n\n$$\na={x}_{0}<{x}_{1}<{x}_{2}<…<{x}_{n}=b\\,.\n$$\n\nWe can express $P$ using set notation as\n\n$$\nP={{x}_{0},{x}_{1},…,{x}_{n}}.\n$$\n\n \n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement:\n\n- Statement I is not a partition of $[-4,6]$ since $-5$ does not belong to $[-4,6].$\n\n- Statement II is not a partition of $[-4,6]$ since it does not include the left endpoint $a=-4.$\n\n- Statement III is not a partition of  $[-4,6]$ since it does not include the right endpoint $b=6.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None of the statements give a partition of  $[-4,6].$\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186396640 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "51d50237-f6cf-5c2d-b8d1-335378205a28" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Convergence of Sequences Containing Exponential Logarithmic Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1c474915-3112-5a65-ba46-8702d62ab30b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${a}_{n}$ and ${c}_{n}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396642} {:answer/id #uuid "fcd4e9ae-e251-5b16-b1c3-5b9bc45cef41" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${a}_{n}$ and ${b}_{n}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396643} {:answer/id #uuid "4bce095d-cbe6-5499-a685-c442389e61af" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${a}_{n},$ ${b}_{n},$ and ${c}_{n}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396644} {:answer/id #uuid "0b06ae43-fe31-53fd-8b10-6540097b006e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${c}_{n}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396645} {:answer/id #uuid "d4a0eaea-fc7b-55da-b356-f336150395ab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${b}_{n}$ and ${c}_{n}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396646}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4bce095d-cbe6-5499-a685-c442389e61af" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${a}_{n},$ ${b}_{n},$ and ${c}_{n}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396644} :answer-field/id #uuid "520b0925-7644-5da3-a57b-98c58f41393e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396641}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "b9bf7b26-4c35-5b3c-bd82-2290add25e67" :question/math-academy-id "q-123715" :question/problem "Which of the following sequences, defined for $n\\ge 1,$ converge?\n\n- ${a}_{n}=\\frac{{log}_{2}⁡(3n)}{{e}^{2n}}$\n- ${b}_{n}=\\frac{{log}_{2}⁡n}{\\sqrt{n}}$\n- ${c}_{n}=\\frac{{n}^{2}}{{e}^{n}}$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that for large values of $n,$ we have\n\n$$\nn!≫{e}^{n}≫{n}^{m}≫\\operatorname{ln}⁡(n),\n$$\n\nwhere $≫$ means \"much greater than,\" and $m>0.$\n\nTo find the limit of each sequence, we must evaluate them for $n\\to \\infty .$\n\nFor ${a}_{n}=\\frac{{log}_{2}⁡(3n)}{{e}^{2n}},$ both the numerator and denominator approach infinity as $n\\to \\infty .$ However, the denominator is growing much faster than the numerator, and we conclude that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{a}_{n}=0.\n$$\n\nFor ${b}_{n}=\\frac{{log}_{2}⁡n}{\\sqrt{n}},$ both the numerator and denominator approach infinity as $n\\to \\infty .$ However, the denominator is growing much faster than the numerator, and we conclude that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{b}_{n}=0.\n$$\n\nFor ${c}_{n}=\\frac{{n}^{2}}{{e}^{n}},$ both the numerator and denominator approach infinity as $n\\to \\infty .$ However, the denominator is growing much faster than the numerator, and we conclude that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{c}_{n}=0.\n$$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"${a}_{n},$ ${b}_{n},$ and ${c}_{n}.$\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186396647 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f98332fd-e902-5390-a4d3-4e181ce97219" :knowledge-point/title "Sets Defined Using Trigonometric Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "08b646ac-4e89-5442-9a96-fda0b2f79464" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396649} {:answer/id #uuid "015a3916-4f00-5a67-a764-b51e236937f0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B$ and $C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396650} {:answer/id #uuid "2f030092-da67-5e23-9fdb-56642eb1665c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $B$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396651} {:answer/id #uuid "c378d3ab-a8f5-57f7-bc3c-e72ba32865fe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396652} {:answer/id #uuid "94c121cc-a02d-5f14-83e4-a6c4f52c26fd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A,$ $B,$ and $C$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396653}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "94c121cc-a02d-5f14-83e4-a6c4f52c26fd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A,$ $B,$ and $C$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396653} :answer-field/id #uuid "cc69c548-b178-59cf-a7e3-c85b144b3d40" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396648}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0e72e5e7-5d89-539b-aadc-437975100b51" :question/math-academy-id "q-181670" :question/problem "Which of the following sets are infinite?\n\n- $A={n\\in Z\\,:\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi }{2})=1}$\n- $B={q\\in Q\\,:\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{q\\pi }{2})=1}$\n- $C={x\\in R\\,:\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{x\\pi }{2})=1}$" :question/worked-solution "The cardinality of a finite set is the number of elements contained within it. A set is infinite if it has infinitely many elements.\n\nWe start by finding the solutions to the equation $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{x\\pi }{2})=1$ for $x\\in R:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x\\pi }{2} & =2m\\pi  \\\\ x & =4m,\\,m\\in Z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our sets:\n\n- The set $A$ is infinite. Listing the elements of $B$ for different values of $m,$ we have\n\n$$\nA={…,-4,0,4,8,…}.\n$$\n\n- The set $B$ is infinite. Since $A$ is infinite, and $A⊆B,$ it follows that $B$ is infinite.\n\n- The set $C$ is infinite. Since $B$ is infinite, and $B⊆C,$ it follows that $C$ is infinite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$A,$ $B,$ and $C.$\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064618 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e901ea71-aeab-5e95-939e-2401558aa708" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Global Extrema of a Function on a Given Interval"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ae1b3034-251a-59f4-b113-773134be1def" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396655} {:answer/id #uuid "b0a7e325-bb6e-50d6-8115-8a687cfd729f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396656} {:answer/id #uuid "b53f607f-c200-5ff6-8ced-e452dc4d4a4b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396657} {:answer/id #uuid "dc8d5f4e-4f95-5ba3-ba2b-62b542d4c351" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396658} {:answer/id #uuid "0481ca8b-da90-5343-99ae-63643fac6ed9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396659}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b0a7e325-bb6e-50d6-8115-8a687cfd729f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396656} :answer-field/id #uuid "041ff950-f3f8-5661-8897-41d87f43abe3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396654}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "542cb4a0-6380-5eaf-91e8-44f9300f10c9" :question/math-academy-id "q-22428" :question/problem "For the function $f(x)=x-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x,$ defined on the closed interval $\\frac{1}{2}\\le x\\le e,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. The global maximum value is $\\frac{1}{2}+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2$\nII. The global minimum value is $e-2$\nIII. The global minimum value is $2-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2$" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "Let's apply the candidates test step by step.\n\n**Step 1**: We find the critical points. Taking the derivative of $f(x)=x-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x,$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=1-\\frac{2}{x},\n$$\n\nand solving ${f}^{′}(x)=0,$ we get \n\n$$\n1-\\frac{2}{x}=0\\,⟹\\,x=2.\n$$\n\nSo, the critical points are $x=2,$ and the endpoints $x=\\frac{1}{2},e$ (since the derivative is not defined at the endpoints).\n\n**Step 2**: We evaluate $f(x)$ at the critical points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\frac{1}{2}) & =\\frac{1}{2}-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{2})=\\frac{1}{2}+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2≈1.9 \\\\ f(2) & =2-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2≈0.6 \\\\ f(e) & =e-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡e=e-2≈0.7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: We compare all of the values found in step $2.$\n\n- The greatest value is $f(\\frac{1}{2})=\\frac{1}{2}+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2,$ so the global maximum is $\\frac{1}{2}+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2.$\n\n- The smallest value is $f(2)=2-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2,$ so the global minimum is $2-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is, \"I and III only.\"\n\n![](images/6c/6cc3ee476c8c5dc9e27051c990b184318147caffd2177b760074172fe5c667f6.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186396735 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4eca32dd-1563-55c6-b594-08588efd601b" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Inhomogeneous System Using a Given Matrix Exponential"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "556a1d6d-0b97-5b9c-919a-ffcaef348e99" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(7t)+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396737} {:answer/id #uuid "47177d5f-d60d-5a76-a622-4448eaae8c59" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(7t)-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396738} {:answer/id #uuid "7b9b3be8-96e2-584f-b6b0-e44dcfa5b59f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(7t)+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396739} {:answer/id #uuid "5e17787c-fdb3-5dc6-9f7d-ad11c18b5f0b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(7t)-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396740}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5e17787c-fdb3-5dc6-9f7d-ad11c18b5f0b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(7t)-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396740} :answer-field/id #uuid "0c3839e0-7572-5b48-a67f-8007a8d31b72" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396736}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9ba8d18b-c5fe-5645-a494-a8fce82f879d" :question/math-academy-id "q-335183" :question/problem "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=7y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=7x(t)+7,\\end{aligned}\\,[\\begin{aligned}x(0) \\\\ y(0)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFind the solution to the initial value problem above.\n\n*Hint: ${e}^{At}=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(7t) & \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(7t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(7t) & \\operatorname{cosh}⁡(7t)\\end{aligned}],$ where $A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 7 \\\\ 7 & 0\\end{aligned}].$*\n\n| $[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | $\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(7t)$ |  |  |" :question/worked-solution "Writing our system in matrix form, we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 7 \\\\ 7 & 0\\end{aligned}]x(t)+g(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]$ and $g(t)=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}].$ So, the matrix of the system is $A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 7 \\\\ 7 & 0\\end{aligned}].$\n\nGiven the system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+g(t),$ where $A$ is a constant square matrix, the solution of the initial value problem can be written as the sum of the homogeneous part and a particular solution:\n\n$$\nx(t)={{{e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})}\\,x({t}_{0})}_{⏟}}_{\\text{homogeneous part}}+{{{\\int }_{{t}_{0}}^{t}{e}^{A(t-s)}g(s)\\,\\text{d}s}_{⏟}}_{\\text{particular solution}},\n$$\n\nwhere $x({t}_{0})$ is the initial condition vector. \n\nLet's evaluate each part separately:\n\n- First, we find the homogeneous part. We are given that \n\n$$\n{e}^{At}=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(7t) & \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(7t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(7t) & \\operatorname{cosh}⁡(7t)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSince the initial conditions are given at $t={t}_{0}=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})} & ={e}^{At}\\,x(0) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(7t) & \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(7t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(7t) & \\operatorname{cosh}⁡(7t)\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Notice that \n\n$$\n{e}^{A(t-s)}=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(7(t-s)) & \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(7(t-s)) \\\\ \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(7(t-s)) & \\operatorname{cosh}⁡(7(t-s))\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{A(t-s)}g(s) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(7(t-s)) & \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(7(t-s)) \\\\ \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(7(t-s)) & \\operatorname{cosh}⁡(7(t-s))\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}7\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(7(t-s)) \\\\ 7\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(7(t-s))\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{t}{e}^{A(t-s)}g(s)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{t}[\\begin{aligned}7\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(7(t-s)) \\\\ 7\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(7(t-s))\\end{aligned}]\\text{d}s \\\\  & ={[\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(7(t-s)) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(7(t-s))\\end{aligned}]}_{s=0}^{s=t} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(7t)-1 \\\\ \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(7t)\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(7t)-1 \\\\ \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(7t)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(7t)-1 \\\\ \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(7t)\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186396741 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4c4b78fa-76af-5261-87a0-d1960aa0fe04" :knowledge-point/title "Proving the Matrix Exponential Formula for IVP Solution of an Inhomogeneous System"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bb2b0c4a-5f41-51b1-b049-b17c6daa263c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "evaluating at $t=0$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396743} {:answer/id #uuid "4db61969-a679-5616-b6d7-9ef5595f083e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "applying a change of variables" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396744} {:answer/id #uuid "b001af69-f2b3-5389-9b56-04a143bc76e2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "substituting ${e}^{At}y(t)=x(t)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396745} {:answer/id #uuid "4fa8e09b-3618-57a8-8bb5-285e3c3130c2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "multiplying both sides by ${e}^{At}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396746}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b001af69-f2b3-5389-9b56-04a143bc76e2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "substituting ${e}^{At}y(t)=x(t)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396745} :answer-field/id #uuid "4423074f-337c-560f-b946-81a6f27cf80f" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396742} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e8b178c4-a799-5c62-8f72-1a528733ac9c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{At}y(t)=x(t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396748} {:answer/id #uuid "c166c192-8884-511b-9ab3-520dfd369763" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(0)=x(0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396749} {:answer/id #uuid "7e8f0ebe-1062-5726-b1bb-015c0a277f9b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+g(t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396750} {:answer/id #uuid "ec37eb47-c21a-54c2-aa96-bd95ec88139f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(t)={e}^{-At}x(t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396751}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c166c192-8884-511b-9ab3-520dfd369763" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(0)=x(0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396749} :answer-field/id #uuid "e16ad664-1e3d-55f3-8058-30911f724f18" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396747} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9b34ccb5-bae6-51a5-9118-171f36032915" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{L1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396753} {:answer/id #uuid "927a0ce8-1c41-5bff-b199-ca47d4f3891f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{L4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396754} {:answer/id #uuid "d4332a13-7707-56e5-9d4c-f85171d9d42e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{L3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396755}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "927a0ce8-1c41-5bff-b199-ca47d4f3891f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{L4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396754} :answer-field/id #uuid "e2d49272-d22a-5953-a915-b76fe91c045b" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396752} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2b5d015d-eaf2-5953-a208-79b9f37e4968" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "evaluating at $t=0$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396757} {:answer/id #uuid "30eef7f5-8b22-5be9-9b66-818bc874eb41" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "applying a change of variables" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396758} {:answer/id #uuid "1f580aa1-90f1-5a47-b37e-9bf7b327dcaa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "substituting ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+g(t)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396759} {:answer/id #uuid "5548b470-04df-52fd-9078-8aee9471580b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "differentiating and using the product rule" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396760}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1f580aa1-90f1-5a47-b37e-9bf7b327dcaa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "substituting ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+g(t)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396759} :answer-field/id #uuid "5b821195-ec1a-56fc-95c0-6d1616e5c0a5" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396756}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b0202c33-02b6-54b1-9971-6d025f9fe504" :question/math-academy-id "q-335401" :question/problem "Consider the inhomogeneous system  ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+g(t),$ where $A$ is a constant square matrix. A derivation of the formula for the solution $x(t)$ passing through $x(0)$ is presented below.\n\n$\\text{L1}:\\,$ Let $y(t)={e}^{-At}x(t)$  \n\n$\\text{L2}:\\,$ ${y}^{′}(t)=-A{e}^{-At}x(t)+{e}^{-At}{x}^{′}(t)$  \n\n$\\text{L3}:\\,$ ${y}^{′}(t)={e}^{-At}g(t)$  \n\n$\\text{L4}:\\,$ $y(t)-y(0)={\\int }_{0}^{t}{e}^{-As}g(s)\\,\\text{d}s$\n\n$\\text{L5}:\\,$ $y(0)=x(0)$ \n\n$\\text{L6}:\\,$ $y(t)=x(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{t}{e}^{-As}g(s)\\,\\text{d}s$  \n\n$\\text{L7}:\\,$ ${e}^{At}y(t)={e}^{At}x(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{t}{e}^{A(t-s)}g(s)\\,\\text{d}s$\n\n$\\text{L8}:\\,$ ${e}^{At}y(t)=x(t)$\n\n$\\text{L9}:\\,$ $x(t)={e}^{At}x(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{t}{e}^{A(t-s)}g(s)\\,\\text{d}s$\n\nSelect the correct options to justify each step of the reasoning.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L3}$ follows from $\\text{L2}$ by  \n{{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L6}$ follows from line\n{{field-2}}\nby substituting \n{{field-3}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L9}$ follows from line $\\text{L7}$ by  \n{{field-4}}." :question/worked-solution "Let's justify each of the selected steps.\n\n- We first consider lines $\\text{L2}$ and $\\text{L3}.$ Starting from\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(t)=-A{e}^{-At}x(t)+{e}^{-At}{x}^{′}(t)\n$$\n\nin $\\text{L2}$ and substituting ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+g(t)$ from the original system gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(t) & =-A{e}^{-At}x(t)+{e}^{-At}(Ax(t)+g(t)) \\\\  & =-A{e}^{-At}x(t)+A{e}^{-At}x(t)+{e}^{-At}g(t) \\\\  & ={e}^{-At}g(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, $\\text{L3}$ follows from $\\text{L2}$ by $\\text{substituting }{x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+g(t).$\n\n- Next, we consider lines $\\text{L4}$ and $\\text{L6}.$ From $\\text{L4}$ we have\n\n$$\ny(t)-y(0)={\\int }_{0}^{t}{e}^{-As}g(s)\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nUsing $y(0)=x(0)$ from $\\text{L5}$, this becomes\n\n$$\ny(t)-x(0)={\\int }_{0}^{t}{e}^{-As}g(s)\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nso\n\n$$\ny(t)=x(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{t}{e}^{-As}g(s)\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nThus, $\\text{L6}$ follows from $\\text{L4}$ by substituting $y(0)=x(0).$\n\n- Finally, we consider lines $\\text{L7}$ and $\\text{L9}.$ From $\\text{L8}$ we know that\n\n$$\n{e}^{At}y(t)=x(t).\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${e}^{At}y(t)=x(t)$ into\n\n$$\n{e}^{At}y(t)={e}^{At}x(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{t}{e}^{A(t-s)}g(s)\\,\\text{d}s\n$$\n\nfrom $\\text{L7}$ gives\n\n$$\nx(t)={e}^{At}x(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{t}{e}^{A(t-s)}g(s)\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\text{L9}$ follows from $\\text{L7}$ by $\\text{substituting }{e}^{At}y(t)=x(t).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186396761 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4c4b78fa-76af-5261-87a0-d1960aa0fe04" :knowledge-point/title "Proving the Matrix Exponential Formula for IVP Solution of an Inhomogeneous System"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6340a434-1f61-5e6a-a285-4fc8d1b9102a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "differentiating and using the sum rule" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396763} {:answer/id #uuid "8b331929-7f99-5db3-bfa8-928f56ab52cc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "multiplying both sides by ${e}^{A(t+{t}_{0})}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396764} {:answer/id #uuid "a9383354-b3ca-54ba-be42-38ef63dbcfa7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "multiplying both sides by ${e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396765} {:answer/id #uuid "11fbce92-a29e-5530-88e3-f9015373285b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "evaluating at $t={t}_{0}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396766}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "11fbce92-a29e-5530-88e3-f9015373285b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "evaluating at $t={t}_{0}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396766} :answer-field/id #uuid "af7ae7ba-6ace-5d3b-a3df-d31722ff9859" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396762} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6c225024-4134-558a-ac74-254995be9458" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "applying a change of variables" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396768} {:answer/id #uuid "aa1af8f3-6b38-5b8d-bad8-3a7e70e14522" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "substituting ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+g(t)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396769} {:answer/id #uuid "5381208c-3cf9-501f-8d6d-9c77cfe29564" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "evaluating at $t={t}_{0}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396770} {:answer/id #uuid "c71be608-0adf-5170-8c99-84e72c9a3b61" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "substituting ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+g(t)+{t}_{0}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396771}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "aa1af8f3-6b38-5b8d-bad8-3a7e70e14522" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "substituting ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+g(t)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396769} :answer-field/id #uuid "c6031269-ba85-5872-aa4c-e64d608aeb30" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396767} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1f0731d0-4386-5750-abab-d09fd58801e0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "multiplying both sides by ${e}^{A(t+{t}_{0})}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396773} {:answer/id #uuid "7aebdae2-bb8c-5fcc-8f5e-cbfc865f92de" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "multiplying both sides by ${e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396774} {:answer/id #uuid "51ead76c-972a-53c4-aafe-40ddf79b5d2b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "integrating both sides" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396775} {:answer/id #uuid "bdb4f772-c3f3-5eda-8d5a-76ce2843a3a7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "evaluating at $t={t}_{0}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396776}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7aebdae2-bb8c-5fcc-8f5e-cbfc865f92de" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "multiplying both sides by ${e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396774} :answer-field/id #uuid "5ec773fb-fcb6-505d-9f93-6d40a41f5157" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396772}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8e9e9aa1-972a-5335-8521-470987b0b354" :question/math-academy-id "q-335420" :question/problem "Consider the inhomogeneous system  ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+g(t),$ where $A$ is a constant square matrix. A derivation of the formula for the solution $x(t)$ passing through $x({t}_{0})$ is presented below.\n\n$\\text{L1}:\\,$ Let $y(t)={e}^{-A(t-{t}_{0})}x(t)$  \n\n$\\text{L2}:\\,$ ${y}^{′}(t)=-A{e}^{-A(t-{t}_{0})}x(t)+{e}^{-A(t-{t}_{0})}{x}^{′}(t)$  \n\n$\\text{L3}:\\,$ ${y}^{′}(t)={e}^{-A(t-{t}_{0})}g(t)$  \n\n$\\text{L4}:\\,$ $y(t)-y({t}_{0})={\\int }_{{t}_{0}}^{t}{e}^{-A(s-{t}_{0})}g(s)\\,\\text{d}s$\n\n$\\text{L5}:\\,$ $y({t}_{0})=x({t}_{0})$ \n\n$\\text{L6}:\\,$ $y(t)=x({t}_{0})+{\\int }_{{t}_{0}}^{t}{e}^{-A(s-{t}_{0})}g(s)\\,\\text{d}s$  \n\n$\\text{L7}:\\,$ ${e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})}y(t)={e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})}x({t}_{0})+{\\int }_{{t}_{0}}^{t}{e}^{A(t-s)}g(s)\\,\\text{d}s$\n\n$\\text{L8}:\\,$ ${e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})}y(t)=x(t)$\n\n$\\text{L9}:\\,$ $x(t)={e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})}x({t}_{0})+{\\int }_{{t}_{0}}^{t}{e}^{A(t-s)}g(s)\\,\\text{d}s$\n\nSelect the correct options to justify each step of the reasoning.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L3}$ follows from $\\text{L2}$ by  \n{{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L5}$ follows from line $\\text{L1}$ by  \n{{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L7}$ follows from line $\\text{L6}$ by  \n{{field-3}}." :question/worked-solution "Let's justify each of the selected steps.\n\n- We first consider lines $\\text{L2}$ and $\\text{L3}.$ Starting from\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(t)=-A{e}^{-A(t-{t}_{0})}x(t)+{e}^{-A(t-{t}_{0})}{x}^{′}(t)\n$$\n\nin $\\text{L2}$ and substituting ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+g(t)$ from the original system gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(t) & =-A{e}^{-A(t-{t}_{0})}x(t)+{e}^{-A(t-{t}_{0})}(Ax(t)+g(t)) \\\\  & =-A{e}^{-A(t-{t}_{0})}x(t)+A{e}^{-A(t-{t}_{0})}x(t)+{e}^{-A(t-{t}_{0})}g(t) \\\\  & ={e}^{-A(t-{t}_{0})}g(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, $\\text{L3}$ follows from $\\text{L2}$ by $\\text{substituting }{x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+g(t).$\n\n- Next, we consider lines $\\text{L1}$ and $\\text{L5}.$ Evaluating $y(t)={e}^{-A(t-{t}_{0})}x(t)$ at $t={t}_{0}$ gives\n\n$$\ny({t}_{0})={e}^{-A({t}_{0}-{t}_{0})}x({t}_{0})=Ix({t}_{0})=x({t}_{0}).\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\text{L5}$ follows from $\\text{L1}$ by $\\text{evaluating at }t={t}_{0}.$\n\n- Finally, we consider lines $\\text{L6}$ and $\\text{L7}.$ Multiplying both sides of\n\n$$\ny(t)=x({t}_{0})+{\\int }_{{t}_{0}}^{t}{e}^{-A(s-{t}_{0})}g(s)\\,ds\n$$\n\nfrom $\\text{L6}$ by ${e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})}y(t) & ={e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})}x({t}_{0})+{e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})}{\\int }_{{t}_{0}}^{t}{e}^{-A(s-{t}_{0})}g(s)\\,\\text{d}s \\\\  & ={e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})}x({t}_{0})+{\\int }_{{t}_{0}}^{t}{e}^{A(t-s)}g(s)\\,\\text{d}s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\text{L7}$ follows from $\\text{L6}$ by $\\text{multiplying both sides by }{e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186396777 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4c4b78fa-76af-5261-87a0-d1960aa0fe04" :knowledge-point/title "Proving the Matrix Exponential Formula for IVP Solution of an Inhomogeneous System"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9530b4e7-da11-5019-bfe8-3c2149892dac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "applying a change of variables" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396779} {:answer/id #uuid "ab035245-455a-59ab-8725-7b2b3dc1b537" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "differentiating and using the product rule" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396780} {:answer/id #uuid "8146923c-9627-55e5-9466-bdd3d14e27c8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "evaluating at $t={t}_{0}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396781} {:answer/id #uuid "fc90c463-3f03-5883-940f-f248e494a4a3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "substituting ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+g(t)+{t}_{0}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396782}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ab035245-455a-59ab-8725-7b2b3dc1b537" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "differentiating and using the product rule" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396780} :answer-field/id #uuid "21df18ce-22e3-5482-85f0-c55067bec64a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396778} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1c21b5ac-b71d-524b-81d0-0ef3b970b6ed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})}y(t)=x(t)+{t}_{0}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396784} {:answer/id #uuid "078d5599-c846-5039-bd9a-02645d761385" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y({t}_{0})=x({t}_{0})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396785} {:answer/id #uuid "414722be-f5ce-5a13-b003-003856e09c08" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(t)={e}^{-A(t-{t}_{0})}x(t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396786} {:answer/id #uuid "8f7dcfe0-2d58-5f8f-a1ec-6818a8f4d297" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+g(t)+{t}_{0}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396787}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "078d5599-c846-5039-bd9a-02645d761385" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y({t}_{0})=x({t}_{0})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396785} :answer-field/id #uuid "db0709dd-b7af-5eba-a79a-d6543cc0bc73" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396783} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "353cd3bb-d381-57af-8c36-f62844de59d4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "differentiating and using the sum rule" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396789} {:answer/id #uuid "321a4ddc-5528-5e6b-b7f9-7d47a93777cc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "multiplying both sides by ${e}^{A(t+{t}_{0})}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396790} {:answer/id #uuid "522ad4b4-3746-5cfb-9fb2-8aab4c9517cc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "multiplying both sides by ${e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396791} {:answer/id #uuid "6363a9f5-c0ba-5662-baa1-b274c296d0e8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "evaluating at $t={t}_{0}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396792}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "522ad4b4-3746-5cfb-9fb2-8aab4c9517cc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "multiplying both sides by ${e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396791} :answer-field/id #uuid "fd5e78af-494a-5d4c-bd14-0efe8866e777" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396788} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "449e233e-966c-5368-ab81-8afbf466bc71" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{L1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396794} {:answer/id #uuid "04fa3eb6-36f3-5879-83e8-a5950409c5d0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{L4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396795} {:answer/id #uuid "4f6cdd58-17c9-5b97-9775-beddb57446ae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{L3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396796} {:answer/id #uuid "4fb7fb5b-f4f6-55a9-8057-b2b401444da8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{L5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396797}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "04fa3eb6-36f3-5879-83e8-a5950409c5d0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{L4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396795} :answer-field/id #uuid "41a55de6-34d3-58e7-8fef-7488002a7430" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396793}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d73d6273-7a31-5998-90dd-91a300f07c9c" :question/math-academy-id "q-335422" :question/problem "Consider the inhomogeneous system  ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+g(t),$ where $A$ is a constant square matrix. A derivation of the formula for the solution $x(t)$ passing through $x({t}_{0})$ is presented below.\n\n$\\text{L1}:\\,$ Let $y(t)={e}^{-A(t-{t}_{0})}x(t)$  \n\n$\\text{L2}:\\,$ ${y}^{′}(t)=-A{e}^{-A(t-{t}_{0})}x(t)+{e}^{-A(t-{t}_{0})}{x}^{′}(t)$  \n\n$\\text{L3}:\\,$ ${y}^{′}(t)={e}^{-A(t-{t}_{0})}g(t)$  \n\n$\\text{L4}:\\,$ $y(t)-y({t}_{0})={\\int }_{{t}_{0}}^{t}{e}^{-A(s-{t}_{0})}g(s)\\,\\text{d}s$\n\n$\\text{L5}:\\,$ $y({t}_{0})=x({t}_{0})$ \n\n$\\text{L6}:\\,$ $y(t)=x({t}_{0})+{\\int }_{{t}_{0}}^{t}{e}^{-A(s-{t}_{0})}g(s)\\,\\text{d}s$  \n\n$\\text{L7}:\\,$ ${e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})}y(t)={e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})}x({t}_{0})+{\\int }_{{t}_{0}}^{t}{e}^{A(t-s)}g(s)\\,\\text{d}s$\n\n$\\text{L8}:\\,$ ${e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})}y(t)=x(t)$\n\n$\\text{L9}:\\,$ $x(t)={e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})}x({t}_{0})+{\\int }_{{t}_{0}}^{t}{e}^{A(t-s)}g(s)\\,\\text{d}s$\n\nSelect the correct options to justify each step of the reasoning.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L2}$ follows from $\\text{L1}$ by  \n{{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L6}$ follows from line\n{{field-2}}\nby substituting \n{{field-3}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L7}$ follows from line $\\text{L6}$ by  \n{{field-4}}." :question/worked-solution "Let's justify each of the selected steps.\n\n- We first consider lines $\\text{L1}$ and $\\text{L2}.$ Differentiating $y(t)={e}^{-A(t-{t}_{0})}x(t)$ and applying the product rule gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(t) & =({e}^{-A(t-{t}_{0})})^{′}x(t)+{e}^{-A(t-{t}_{0})}{x}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =(-A){e}^{-A(t-{t}_{0})}x(t)+{e}^{-A(t-{t}_{0})}{x}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =-A{e}^{-A(t-{t}_{0})}x(t)+{e}^{-A(t-{t}_{0})}{x}^{′}(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, $\\text{L2}$ follows from $\\text{L1}$ by $\\text{differentiating and using the product rule}.$\n\n- Next, we consider lines $\\text{L4}$, $\\text{L5}$, and $\\text{L6}.$ From $\\text{L4}$ we have\n\n$$\ny(t)-y({t}_{0})={\\int }_{{t}_{0}}^{t}{e}^{-A(s-{t}_{0})}g(s)\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nUsing $y({t}_{0})=x({t}_{0})$ from $\\text{L5}$, this becomes\n\n$$\ny(t)-x({t}_{0})={\\int }_{{t}_{0}}^{t}{e}^{-A(s-{t}_{0})}g(s)\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nso\n\n$$\ny(t)=x({t}_{0})+{\\int }_{{t}_{0}}^{t}{e}^{-A(s-{t}_{0})}g(s)\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nThus, $\\text{L6}$ follows from $\\text{L4}$ by substituting $y({t}_{0})=x({t}_{0}).$\n\n- Finally, we consider lines $\\text{L6}$ and $\\text{L7}.$ Multiplying both sides of\n\n$$\ny(t)=x({t}_{0})+{\\int }_{{t}_{0}}^{t}{e}^{-A(s-{t}_{0})}g(s)\\,ds\n$$\n\nfrom $\\text{L6}$ by ${e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})}y(t) & ={e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})}x({t}_{0})+{e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})}{\\int }_{{t}_{0}}^{t}{e}^{-A(s-{t}_{0})}g(s)\\,\\text{d}s \\\\  & ={e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})}x({t}_{0})+{\\int }_{{t}_{0}}^{t}{e}^{A(t-s)}g(s)\\,\\text{d}s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\text{L7}$ follows from $\\text{L6}$ by $\\text{multiplying both sides by }{e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186396798 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ffe4292f-7653-5486-975e-c206acae734e" :knowledge-point/title "Applying Properties of Big-O"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e527463f-8290-5ced-ad81-399453d89115" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the absorption property" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396800} {:answer/id #uuid "d06cc1de-4117-5a41-b415-fe4170c4524d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the constant multiple property" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396801} {:answer/id #uuid "412b8550-fc4a-59a3-8ac2-9783dfa74f8c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the sum property" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396802} {:answer/id #uuid "d7a6d35c-3367-5826-9041-f6ef5d13a984" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the transitive property" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396803} {:answer/id #uuid "33ab1682-da7e-55da-9530-85ffff2748d7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the reflexive property" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396804}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "412b8550-fc4a-59a3-8ac2-9783dfa74f8c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the sum property" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396802} :answer-field/id #uuid "76168965-fb6c-530a-8fbd-b5a10cd9db80" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396799} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b1871747-f434-5892-a1e7-0be2f34b861f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\text{L2}$ and $\\text{L3}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396806} {:answer/id #uuid "d4844669-5a1a-5895-a057-e84a7a99200c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\text{L1}$ and $\\text{L3}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396807}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d4844669-5a1a-5895-a057-e84a7a99200c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\text{L1}$ and $\\text{L3}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396807} :answer-field/id #uuid "4f87f2a9-0746-57ea-b114-4b2350a6b086" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396805} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ff8faea-f827-5adb-b33b-48e91101e498" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the absorption property" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396809} {:answer/id #uuid "2d106926-150f-523f-af6c-e8ad34a70096" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the constant multiple property" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396810} {:answer/id #uuid "e5c70c02-ec4f-59d4-9bf9-2e622c38fff6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the sum property" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396811} {:answer/id #uuid "076ef44f-d525-5f3d-bced-572d99723819" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the transitive  property" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396812} {:answer/id #uuid "0cb64f04-559f-5a08-9f7f-c1e0373484da" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the reflexive property" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396813}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2d106926-150f-523f-af6c-e8ad34a70096" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the constant multiple property" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396810} :answer-field/id #uuid "6cb5a7c0-71c0-543a-9a51-5b97dd34d5a9" :answer-field/key "field-5" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396808} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1ae6b545-b77c-5d5b-9931-736b82549b31" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the absorption property" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396815} {:answer/id #uuid "89424f11-45a5-55bd-b37e-893709471e0c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the constant multiple property" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396816} {:answer/id #uuid "2cbb751e-a325-5db5-b363-985130d0a919" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the sum property" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396817} {:answer/id #uuid "975ab894-9921-5186-81fe-4c114ee83d5c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the transitive property" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396818} {:answer/id #uuid "6d9cbd1d-e481-5685-ac5d-c46ede74e55e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the reflexive property" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396819}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6d9cbd1d-e481-5685-ac5d-c46ede74e55e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the reflexive property" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396819} :answer-field/id #uuid "12adf0fb-a2ca-581e-a5b3-29d3bd797f25" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396814} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "210f1777-cf42-569d-a917-65499a15c777" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{L1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396821} {:answer/id #uuid "1b87a34c-7c82-5aee-b376-23612b63939e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{L4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396822} {:answer/id #uuid "49ad5f41-7687-51cb-ba4a-590a4318edc4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{L3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396823}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1b87a34c-7c82-5aee-b376-23612b63939e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{L4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396822} :answer-field/id #uuid "53fe5cf4-ec62-5c60-b7a4-8115f70289d6" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396820}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e15b51c9-b3ac-5fdf-bdd1-db7479e393a2" :question/math-academy-id "q-335438" :question/problem "Consider the following statement. \n\nIf $f(x)=3x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x,$ then $f=O(x)$ as $x\\to 0.$\n\nA proof of the statement is given below.\n\n$\\text{L1}:\\,3x=O(3x)$\n\n$\\text{L2}:\\,{lim}_{x\\to 0}(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x}{2x})=1$\n\n$\\text{L3}:\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x=O(2x)$ as $x\\to 0$\n\n$\\text{L4}:\\,f=O(5x)$ as $x\\to 0$ \n\n$\\text{L5}:\\,f=O(x)$ as $x\\to 0$\n\nSelect the correct options to justify each step of the reasoning.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L1}$ follows from {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L4}$ follows from lines \n{{field-2}} and \n{{field-3}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L5}$ follows from line {{field-4}}  and \n{{field-5}}." :question/worked-solution "Suppose $f(x),g(x),h(x)$ and $k(x)$ are functions and $c$ is a nonzero constant. Then, we have the following properties of Big-O notation. \n\n- The sum and product properties. If\n\n$$\nf=O(h)\\,\\text{and}\\,g=O(k)\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a\n$$\n\nthen\n\n$$\nf+g=O(h+k)\\,\\text{and}\\,fg=O(hk)\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a.\n$$\n\n- The constant multiple property. If\n\n$$\nf=O(cg)\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a,\n$$\n\nthen\n\n$$\nf=O(g)\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a.\n$$\n\n- The reflexive property.\n\n$$\nf=O(f)\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a.\n$$\n\n- The transitive property. If\n\n$$\nf=O(g)\\,\\text{and}\\,g=O(h)\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a,\n$$\n\nthen\n\n$$\nf=O(h)\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a.\n$$\n\n- The absorption property. If\n\n$$\nf=O(g+h)\\,\\text{and}\\,g=O(h)\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a,\n$$\n\nthen\n\n$$\nf=O(h)\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let us justify each of the selected steps.\n\n- We first consider line $\\text{L1},$ which states that\n\n$$\n3x=O({{3x}_{⏟}}_{f}).\n$$\n\nThis is true by $\\text{the reflexive property}.$\n\n- Next, we consider line $\\text{L4}.$ From line $\\text{L1}$ we have\n\n$$\n3x=O(3x),\n$$\n\nand from line $\\text{L3}$ we have\n\n$$\n-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x=O(2x)\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to 0.\n$$\n\nThus, by lines $\\text{L1 and L3}$ and $\\text{the sum property},$ we obtain\n\n$$\nf=O(5x)\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to 0.\n$$\n\n- Finally, we consider line $\\text{L5}.$ From line  $\\text{L4}$   we have\n\n$$\nf=O(5x)\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to 0,\n$$\n\nand by the $\\text{constant multiple property}$ with $c=5,$ we have\n\n$$\nf=O(x)\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to 0.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186396824 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ffe4292f-7653-5486-975e-c206acae734e" :knowledge-point/title "Applying Properties of Big-O"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "35c14a88-2f8e-50c7-a553-cce91615d84d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{L4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396826} {:answer/id #uuid "bffdbb57-4fd0-54cf-b6f9-dc38b7998642" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{L2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396827} {:answer/id #uuid "84d83159-e6f7-5dcb-96ee-01f76997a673" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{L3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396828}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "35c14a88-2f8e-50c7-a553-cce91615d84d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{L4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396826} :answer-field/id #uuid "cc543933-c02b-591f-8cf5-11706b38483b" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396825} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a0171ac5-2979-51d5-8963-edfbcd9126a0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the absorption property" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396830} {:answer/id #uuid "6f3f8806-972d-5465-9195-f699121c2965" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the constant multiple property" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396831} {:answer/id #uuid "3f5d818a-1c81-53fe-85eb-42fb5d104302" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the sum property" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396832} {:answer/id #uuid "7336c43b-083b-5aca-9d74-a1c4fbc23c0c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the transitive property" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396833} {:answer/id #uuid "25be9492-14c1-5151-bd2a-f48309b37689" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the reflexive property" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396834}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a0171ac5-2979-51d5-8963-edfbcd9126a0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the absorption property" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396830} :answer-field/id #uuid "e018cb38-5370-53d0-b6a4-410327d4f73b" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396829} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "aa92b838-82ae-5998-8890-3edccb9454f0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the absorption property" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396836} {:answer/id #uuid "9422130e-7d41-59c0-9c3b-e82a870fd3e0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the constant multiple property" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396837} {:answer/id #uuid "b10d5f73-5ec3-5ec2-a7e2-6bdd3b13d3dd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the sum property" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396838} {:answer/id #uuid "fedcf2a1-58c2-5fe0-abf5-3ba83b146f86" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the transitive property" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396839} {:answer/id #uuid "ff395391-34ba-5fcb-a35c-97965c0d981e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the reflexive property" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396840}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9422130e-7d41-59c0-9c3b-e82a870fd3e0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the constant multiple property" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396837} :answer-field/id #uuid "e3bbb676-81e2-50e3-a3bb-87f4e8b01e41" :answer-field/key "field-5" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396835} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "adcbfc07-80e8-5ea1-b84e-13f16a7e0b7a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the absorption property" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396842} {:answer/id #uuid "9bb130c3-0aff-5067-91a1-ee45706f1245" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the constant multiple property" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396843} {:answer/id #uuid "8054b0b3-df38-5516-9370-a63b32150442" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the sum property" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396844} {:answer/id #uuid "8f721fc4-0680-57ca-bbe6-1e1378545e6c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the transitive property" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396845} {:answer/id #uuid "b80f8558-60e8-5dc7-9392-0ec85bf1101f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the reflexive property" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396846}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b80f8558-60e8-5dc7-9392-0ec85bf1101f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the reflexive property" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396846} :answer-field/id #uuid "7b22cd9c-5cf4-532f-a1ca-970ec9a3d464" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396841} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "096d8579-05d7-55b7-ab38-419cac3562a5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{L2 and L3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396848} {:answer/id #uuid "8ebff648-bb6f-5ff2-84af-8d82f7000bf3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{L1 and L3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396849}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8ebff648-bb6f-5ff2-84af-8d82f7000bf3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{L1 and L3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396849} :answer-field/id #uuid "4d4d43f0-56f8-5977-b48a-2665291f0625" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396847}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ac4293b7-4265-5467-bc7b-b86d5611daed" :question/math-academy-id "q-335445" :question/problem "Consider the following statement. \n\nIf $f(x)=2+{e}^{-x},$ then $f=O(1)$ as $x\\to \\infty .$\n\nA proof of the statement is given below.\n\n$\\text{L1}:\\,f=O(2+{e}^{-x})$  \n\n$\\text{L2}:\\,{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{{e}^{-x}}{2}=0$  \n\n$\\text{L3}:\\,{e}^{-x}=O(2)$  \n\n$\\text{L4}:\\,f=O(2)$  \n\n$\\text{L5}:\\,f=O(1)$  \n\nSelect the correct options to justify each step of the reasoning.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L1}$ follows from  \n{{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L4}$ follows from lines  \n{{field-2}}  \nand  \n{{field-3}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L5}$ follows from line  \n{{field-4}}  \nand  \n{{field-5}}." :question/worked-solution "Suppose $f(x),g(x),h(x)$ and $k(x)$ are functions and $c$ is a nonzero constant. Then, we have the following properties of Big-O notation. \n\n- The sum and product properties. If\n\n$$\nf=O(h)\\,\\text{and}\\,g=O(k)\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a\n$$\n\n \nthen\n\n$$\nf+g=O(h+k)\\,\\text{and}\\,fg=O(hk)\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a.\n$$\n\n- The constant multiple property. If \n\n$$\nf=O(cg)\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a\n$$\n\n \nthen\n\n$$\nf=O(g)\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a.\n$$\n\n- The reflexive property. \n\n$$\nf=O(f)\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a\n$$\n\n- The transitive property. If \n\n$$\nf=O(g)\\,\\text{and}\\,g=O(h)\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a\n$$\n\nthen\n\n$$\nf=O(h)\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a.\n$$\n\n- The absorption property. If\n\n$$\nf=O(g+h)\\,\\text{and}\\,g=O(h)\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a\n$$\n\n \nthen \n\n$$\nf=O(h)\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's justify each of the selected steps.\n\n- We first consider line $\\text{L1},$ which states that\n\n$$\nf=O({{2+{e}^{-x}}_{⏟}}_{f}),\n$$\n\nand this is true by the $\\text{reflexive property}.$\n\n- Next, we consider line $\\text{L4}.$ From line $\\text{L1}$ we have\n\n$$\nf=O(2+{e}^{-x}),\n$$\n\nand from line $\\text{L3}$ we have\n\n$$\n{e}^{-x}=O(2).\n$$\n\nThus, by lines   $\\text{L1 and L3}$ and the  $\\text{absorption property},$ we obtain\n\n$$\nf=O(2).\n$$\n\n- Finally, we consider line $\\text{L5}.$ From line $\\text{L4},$ we have\n\n$$\nf=O(2)\n$$\n\nand by the $\\text{constant multiple property}$ with $c=2,$ we have\n\n$$\nf=O(1).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186396850 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e4025650-a2f6-597c-9bba-8008d90aee67" :knowledge-point/title "Understanding Definitions of Stability Types"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ddec3d21-d9a8-5b05-8b8b-bf5789fceefb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "arbitrarily close to" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396852} {:answer/id #uuid "2ddafa6a-7da8-507f-81ed-08fd9d2db8ad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "far from" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396853}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ddec3d21-d9a8-5b05-8b8b-bf5789fceefb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "arbitrarily close to" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396852} :answer-field/id #uuid "4fae1d8c-606a-5bd4-a555-2a631ad7f437" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396851} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3edfa3a9-5202-50e5-bd72-0e10a5d47289" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "All" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396855} {:answer/id #uuid "4ec4c355-4b8a-576a-a899-56bcc23c083e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Some" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396856}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4ec4c355-4b8a-576a-a899-56bcc23c083e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Some" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396856} :answer-field/id #uuid "06642795-f3b2-5a1d-8756-9f9c0fba8344" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396854} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "57360b16-4b38-50df-85d1-531bb626e68a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "remain close to" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396858} {:answer/id #uuid "0c02ae13-acc9-5b84-b898-d987d50404ef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "eventually move away from" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396859}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0c02ae13-acc9-5b84-b898-d987d50404ef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "eventually move away from" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396859} :answer-field/id #uuid "58ab2b4c-0421-57e3-9439-37e1ee616b4e" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396857}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d4f31534-294a-5c66-bb7a-bbd9c8ed5e89" :question/math-academy-id "q-335460" :question/problem "Select the correct options in the statement below that describe an **unstable** equilibrium point.{{field-1}} solutions that start\n{{field-2}}\nthe equilibrium\n{{field-3}}\nthe equilibrium." :question/worked-solution "Let's recall the definitions of stability:\n\n- An equilibrium point ${x}^{∗}$ is called *stable* if for any neighborhood $O$ of ${x}^{∗},$ there exists a neighborhood ${O}_{1}⊆O$ such that every orbit that begins inside ${O}_{1}$ at some point in time $t={t}_{0}$ remains inside $O$ for all $t>{t}_{0}.$\n\nIn plain words, this means that solutions that start sufficiently close to the equilibrium remain close, but may not converge to the equilibrium.\n\n- An equilibrium point ${x}^{∗}$ is called *asymptotically stable* if it is stable and there exists a neighborhood $O$ of ${x}^{∗}$ such that every orbit that begins inside $O$ approaches ${x}^{∗}$ as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nIn plain words, this means that solutions that start sufficiently close to the equilibrium remain close and converge to the equilibrium as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- An equilibrium point ${x}^{∗}$ is called *unstable* if it is not stable.\n\nIn plain words, this means that solutions eventually move away from the equilibrium for some initial conditions arbitrarily close to it.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is the following:\n\n$\\text{Some}$ solutions that start $\\text{arbitrarily close to}$ the equilibrium $\\text{eventually move away from}$ the equilibrium."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186396860 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e4025650-a2f6-597c-9bba-8008d90aee67" :knowledge-point/title "Understanding Definitions of Stability Types"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7acacba1-6f69-50e7-9864-e82f875f3e4c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "close to" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396862} {:answer/id #uuid "31ed6079-395e-5ee2-ba11-4ffbcddbe9aa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "away from" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396863} {:answer/id #uuid "b985aed1-7358-5e7b-ab66-d0a4361f12c2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "exactly at" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396864}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7acacba1-6f69-50e7-9864-e82f875f3e4c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "close to" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396862} :answer-field/id #uuid "dd666725-4037-50ad-9322-132e6584f76a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396861} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f797104a-b169-5e09-845d-5f807e277b2a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "approach and move away from" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396866} {:answer/id #uuid "1f4783cf-2a80-559e-b115-4da84ec3ff7a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "remain close to" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396867} {:answer/id #uuid "ab06307b-561e-561b-bc5b-ec96a6bebff5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "eventually move away from" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396868}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1f4783cf-2a80-559e-b115-4da84ec3ff7a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "remain close to" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396867} :answer-field/id #uuid "7d867b79-f420-59a0-b427-5d7e13137929" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396865} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "20612cc0-84ee-5b88-a26f-470820172fa9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "may not converge to the equilibrium as $t\\to \\infty $" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396870} {:answer/id #uuid "671730d5-989d-55ab-a270-ae825f4fbb30" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "always converge to the equilibrium as $t\\to \\infty $" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396871} {:answer/id #uuid "9898c13e-f508-5911-928d-68e0d683195d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "never converge to the equilibrium as $t\\to \\infty $" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396872}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "671730d5-989d-55ab-a270-ae825f4fbb30" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "always converge to the equilibrium as $t\\to \\infty $" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396871} :answer-field/id #uuid "fd4a8bc6-57d0-51df-b61d-7a76918f0898" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396869}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2ab9c96d-932f-5272-8662-18e0a52dc3ed" :question/math-academy-id "q-335463" :question/problem "Select the correct options in the statement below that describe an **asymptotically stable** equilibrium point.The solutions that start sufficiently\n{{field-1}}\nthe equilibrium\n{{field-2}}\nit for all future time, and\n{{field-3}}." :question/worked-solution "Let's recall the definitions of stability:\n\n- An equilibrium point ${x}^{∗}$ is called *stable* if for any neighborhood $O$ of ${x}^{∗},$ there exists a neighborhood ${O}_{1}⊆O$ such that every orbit that begins inside ${O}_{1}$ at some point in time $t={t}_{0}$ remains inside $O$ for all $t>{t}_{0}.$\n\nIn plain words, this means that solutions that start sufficiently close to the equilibrium remain close, but may not converge to the equilibrium.\n\n- An equilibrium point ${x}^{∗}$ is called *asymptotically stable* if it is stable and there exists a neighborhood $O$ of ${x}^{∗}$ such that every orbit that begins inside $O$ approaches ${x}^{∗}$ as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nIn plain words, this means that solutions that start sufficiently close to the equilibrium remain close and converge to the equilibrium as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- An equilibrium point ${x}^{∗}$ is called *unstable* if it is not stable.\n\nIn plain words, this means that solutions eventually move away from the equilibrium for some initial conditions arbitrarily close to it.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is the following:\n\nThe solutions that start sufficiently $\\text{close to}$ the equilibrium $\\text{remain close to}$ it for all future time, and $\\text{always converge to the equilibrium as }t\\to \\infty .$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186396873 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e4025650-a2f6-597c-9bba-8008d90aee67" :knowledge-point/title "Understanding Definitions of Stability Types"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3abae5d3-02ce-535c-bb22-07a9d6832cf9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "reach" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396875} {:answer/id #uuid "64a6e9e1-8142-5c95-9768-8b45c83b4495" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "move away from" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396876}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid 