type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397064} {:answer/id #uuid "bcae8dee-48c8-50be-9a9d-dd37d0130528" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397065} {:answer/id #uuid "59738da9-7945-56e4-9208-a9ec7560aaa5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x{e}^{-4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397066}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bcae8dee-48c8-50be-9a9d-dd37d0130528" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397065} :answer-field/id #uuid "6be5a4a4-18b9-5519-8e71-87176119df5c" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397060}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "60c74d98-62e0-535f-ae2c-bd22a02ff3d1" :question/math-academy-id "q-337466" :question/problem "Consider the first-order linear ODE\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+4y={e}^{-4x}.\n$$\n\nWe wish to solve this equation using the method of variation of parameters (VOP).\n\nSelect the correct options below.\n\n$\\,$ According to VOP, we assume a particular solution of the form {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ Solving for $u,$ we have $u(x)=$ {{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Therefore, the particular solution is ${y}_{p}(x)=$ {{field-3}}." :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}+4{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{-4x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nThe method of variation of parameters constructs a particular solution by varying the constant in the homogeneous solution. So, to find a suitable ${y}_{p},$ we set $A=u(x)$ in the complementary solution, as given by the following ansatz:\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=u(x){e}^{-4x}\n$$\n\nIntuitively, this form of ${y}_{p}$ tracks the homogeneous solution but with a varying amplitude.\n\nTo find $u,$ we substitute our ansatz into the equation, apply the product rule to the derivative term, and simplify to isolate ${u}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(u{e}^{-4x})}^{′}+4(u{e}^{-4x}) & ={e}^{-4x} \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{-4x}-4u{e}^{-4x}+4u{e}^{-4x} & ={e}^{-4x} \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{-4x}-\\cancel{4u{e}^{-4x}}+\\cancel{4u{e}^{-4x}} & ={e}^{-4x} \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{-4x} & ={e}^{-4x} \\\\ {u}^{′} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides with respect to $x$ (omitting the arbitrary constant), we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′} & =1 \\\\ \\int {u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\,\\text{d}x \\\\ u & =x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${y}_{p}=u(x){e}^{-4x},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p} & =u(x){e}^{-4x} \\\\  & =x{e}^{-4x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186397067 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a5fb547a-2518-5db9-b1b1-aab8d80328af" :knowledge-point/title "Solving First-Order Linear ODEs Using VOP When the Forcing Term is Part of the Complementary Solution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e403ce47-06c5-5e9b-ab8e-462b81d03f09" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x{e}^{-7x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397069} {:answer/id #uuid "11a32a7f-7b9c-504f-b1d3-2e76cef86def" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{7x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397070} {:answer/id #uuid "96b52573-5826-593c-8432-290b29b2304f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-7x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397071} {:answer/id #uuid "0ff820c1-181b-5e4b-acbc-aa742b9a3b31" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x{e}^{7x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397072} {:answer/id #uuid "1ea5bfac-c767-5b9d-ab68-68b54a6358b4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :an