db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{7x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397083} {:answer/id #uuid "b7470fb6-388e-532f-839c-4263ebaddc37" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-7x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397084} {:answer/id #uuid "9448a899-f5a3-5f84-b46b-10313439f92c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x{e}^{7x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397085}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7665d218-e973-5ccf-a112-1b3cee61a4b6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x{e}^{-7x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397082} :answer-field/id #uuid "7d0b526f-0a36-505c-bd42-a28d844cfb3c" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397081}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "34254624-9c90-5339-836d-8f0fcd78b2d0" :question/math-academy-id "q-337469" :question/problem "Consider the first-order linear ODE\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+7y={e}^{-7x}.\n$$\n\nWe wish to solve this equation using the method of variation of parameters (VOP).\n\nSelect the correct options below.\n\n$\\,$ According to VOP, we assume a particular solution of the form {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ Solving for $u,$ we have $u(x)=$ {{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Therefore, the particular solution is ${y}_{p}(x)=$ {{field-3}}." :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}+7{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{-7x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nThe method of variation of parameters constructs a particular solution by varying the constant in the homogeneous solution. So, to find a suitable ${y}_{p},$ we set $A=u(x)$ in the complementary solution, as given by the following ansatz:\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=u(x){e}^{-7x}\n$$\n\nIntuitively, this form of ${y}_{p}$ tracks the homogeneous solution but with a varying amplitude.\n\nTo find $u,$ we substitute our ansatz into the equation, apply the product rule to the derivative term, and simplify to isolate ${u}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(u{e}^{-7x})}^{′}+7(u{e}^{-7x}) & ={e}^{-7x} \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{-7x}-7u{e}^{-7x}+7u{e}^{-7x} & ={e}^{-7x} \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{-7x}-\\cancel{7u{e}^{-7x}}+\\cancel{7u{e}^{-7x}} & ={e}^{-7x} \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{-7x} & ={e}^{-7x} \\\\ {u}^{′} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides with respect to $x$ (omitting the arbitrary constant), we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′} & =1 \\\\ \\int {u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\,\\text{d}x \\\\ u & =x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${y}_{p}=u(x){e}^{-7x},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p} & =u(x){e}^{-7x} \\\\  & =x{e}^{-7x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186397086 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a5fb547a-2518-5db9-b1b1-aab8d80328af" :knowledge-point/title "Solving First-Order Linear ODEs Using VOP When the Forcing Term is Part of the Complementary Solution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "06d45bf0-b0d2-526d-9ee8-966bee2bf82f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5x{e}^{5x}-{e}^{-5x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397088} {:answer/id #uuid "f899948d-7d57-564f-9c17-dc1a4e8c1e4b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{5x}-x{e}^{-5x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397089} {:answer/id #uuid "cd83b01b-26af-5c69-90f0-dea06d731251" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{5x}-\\frac{1}{2}x{e}^{-5x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397090} {:answer/id #uuid "ba369324-b632-52dd-b087-db8abad2c3bf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5x{e}^{5x}-\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{-5x}" :db/id #edb/ref 1759218639709