 #uuid "03257e03-c6ac-58ac-bb14-ad066e5d197b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)⟩}{⟨f(x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)⟩}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397457}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c916f828-527d-56b1-a7f4-05bfb34ceabb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{⟨f(x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)⟩}{⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)⟩}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397455} :answer-field/id #uuid "7c16f558-7e63-5623-83b7-64f167dc11d9" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397452} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2023ddfa-d889-50c9-878f-85d10c84ea35" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397459} {:answer/id #uuid "50c89892-3614-52d6-882b-bc4708a1c3b9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397460} {:answer/id #uuid "ac65746c-c51f-545e-b99a-4117e9bf4102" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397461} {:answer/id #uuid "b5530228-de94-5b2c-b5a7-3602545b76b6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397462} {:answer/id #uuid "57843cbf-6acc-57b7-b1e7-b524e63e1320" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397463}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b5530228-de94-5b2c-b5a7-3602545b76b6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397462} :answer-field/id #uuid "a1d13c1c-619d-5f29-9d08-2c83813e46e8" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397458}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "13f82e1b-2f3e-536a-8bb2-1fc6ddf6b76a" :question/math-academy-id "q-340645" :question/problem "Let $F(x)={x}^{4}-7x$ for $x\\in R,$ and let $f(x)$ be the $2\\pi $-periodic extension of $F$ from $x\\in [0,2\\pi )$ to $x\\in R.$ The Fourier series of $f$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }({a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)).\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options in the expression for the coefficient ${a}_{3}$ below, where $⟨\\,,⟩$ denotes the usual inner product on functions over $[0,2\\pi ).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{3} & ={{field-1}} \\\\  & ={{field-2}}\\,\\cdot \\,{{field-3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The usual inner product of functions $f$ and $g$ over $x\\in [0,2\\pi )$ is given by\n\n$$\n⟨f(x),g(x)⟩={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }f(x)g(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nFor a $2\\pi $-periodic function $f(x)$ that is piecewise continuous over $x\\in [0,2\\pi ),$ the Fourier series of $f$ is given by \n\n$$\nf(x)∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }({a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)),\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{0},$ ${a}_{n},$ and ${b}_{n}$ are constants to be determined. \n\nNow, we find the inner product of $f(x)$ with $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨f(x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)⟩ & = \\\\ ⟨\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }({a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)⟩ & = \\\\ ⟨\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2},\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)⟩+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }⟨{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)⟩+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }⟨{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)⟩ & = \\\\ \\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}⟨1,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)⟩+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)⟩+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the functions\n$1,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x),…$\nare orthogonal with respect to the defined inner product, the only nonzero term in the final expression above is\n\n$$\n{a}_{3}⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)⟩.\n$$\n\nComputing the inner product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)⟩ & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{cos}^{2}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{1}{2}x+\\frac{1}{12}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6x)]}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(2\\pi -0)-\\frac{1}{12}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(12\\pi )-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨f(x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)⟩ & ={a}_{3}⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)⟩ \\\\ {a}_{3} & =\\frac{⟨f(x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)⟩}{⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)⟩} \\\\ {a}_{3} & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }f(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }({x}^{4}-7x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186397464 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c086bc97-415d-52c9-b26a-02dc8aebef33" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Laplace Transforms of Trigonometric and Hyperbolic Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6154e534-d5aa-500e-a111-0642cb1f5138" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "s>3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397466} {:answer/id #uuid "90e7fe06-494b-5336-8647-cec5893e79b9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "s\\ge 3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397467} {:answer/id #uuid "62aa5026-98f2-56d7-9078-cf1f425f67e3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "s\\ge 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397468} {:answer/id #uuid "309debb7-997b-556d-8512-eacab65daa9e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "s>-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397469} {:answer/id #uuid "f9cdc23e-3f2e-58ea-8f36-763705ca4acc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "s>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397470} {:answer/id #uuid "0da6a2a1-bbda-52c4-af89-8ae0dc014a0d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "s\\ge -3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397471}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6154e534-d5aa-500e-a111-0642cb1f5138" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "s>3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397466} :answer-field/id #uuid "8fe74c7f-d29a-5023-a627-6b99c33c73b4" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397465} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9c12b77e-d15a-5d68-b82e-75459493d093" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{{s}^{2}-9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397473} {:answer/id #uuid "375df8b6-49b7-502b-a948-207f0f617763" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{{s}^{2}+9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397474} {:answer/id #uuid "e9e087e4-24d7-5ffa-92ca-31063530bd9b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3s}{{s}^{2}-9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397475} {:answer/id #uuid "e24a7d49-39e9-59b8-a286-404d727d74eb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397476} {:answer/id #uuid "8608c77e-449a-5d7d-bd75-65fa63256437" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}-9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397477}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8608c77e-449a-5d7d-bd75-65fa63256437" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}-9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397477} :answer-field/id #uuid "4dfa2d25-b9ae-5bb9-b792-43292f1be3e4" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397472}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "42bf59e6-fc11-5187-a3f2-889c117ed67e" :question/math-academy-id "q-340655" :question/problem "Use the Laplace transform of $f(t)={e}^{kt}$ to find the Laplace transform of $\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(3t).$$\\,L{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(3t)}=$ {{field-1}},\ndefined for {{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "The linearity property of the Laplace transform states that for functions $f(t)$ and $g(t),$ we have\n\n$$\nL{\\alpha f(t)+\\beta g(t)}=\\alpha \\cdot L{f(t)}+\\beta \\cdot L{g(t)},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants.\n\nFirst, recall that, by definition\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(3t)=\\frac{{e}^{3t}+{e}^{-3t}}{2}.\n$$\n\nTaking the Laplace transform and applying the linearity property, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(3t)} & =L{\\frac{{e}^{3t}+{e}^{-3t}}{2}} \\\\  & =L{\\frac{{e}^{3t}}{2}+\\frac{{e}^{-3t}}{2}} \\\\  & =L{\\frac{{e}^{3t}}{2}}+L{\\frac{{e}^{-3t}}{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}L{{e}^{3t}}+\\frac{1}{2}L{{e}^{-3t}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we recall that \n\n$$\nL{{e}^{kt}}=\\frac{1}{s-k},\\,s>k.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{e}^{3t}} & =\\frac{1}{s-3},\\,s>3 \\\\ L{{e}^{-3t}} & =\\frac{1}{s+3},\\,s>-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Laplace transform of $\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(3t)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(3t)} & =\\frac{1}{2}L{{e}^{3t}}+\\frac{1}{2}L{{e}^{-3t}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{s-3}+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{s+3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{1}{s-3}+\\frac{1}{s+3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{(s+3)+(s-3)}{(s-3)(s+3)}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{2s}{{s}^{2}-9}) \\\\  & =\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}-9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $L{{e}^{3t}}$ is defined for $s\\in (3,\\infty )$ and $L{{e}^{-3t}}$ is defined for $s\\in (-3,\\infty ),$ we have that $L{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(3t)}$ is defined for\n\n$$\n(3,\\infty )\\cap (-3,\\infty )=(3,\\infty ),\n$$\n\nthat is, $s>3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186397478 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fadf4f64-3569-56b2-98e1-f6681892054a" :knowledge-point/title "Checking Orthogonality of Sines and Cosines"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8433c533-4904-5d92-885f-bbcd959dd940" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "are orthogonal" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397480} {:answer/id #uuid "7f4020d2-476a-5794-a6f2-3d8b125330ae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "are not orthogonal" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397481}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8433c533-4904-5d92-885f-bbcd959dd940" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "are orthogonal" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397480} :answer-field/id #uuid "199c37d6-b74a-50da-a86e-fce04acf3561" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397479} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "deda8e08-d6bd-5e0e-ae0c-00112e17523f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397483} {:answer/id #uuid "4d231c1e-d79b-5508-b44e-84b13e3b138c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397484} {:answer/id #uuid "2134b48b-4f80-54ce-97ca-e2ded504ec18" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397485} {:answer/id #uuid "dfbe5295-15de-5429-ac7d-1288d0b2be34" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397486}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2134b48b-4f80-54ce-97ca-e2ded504ec18" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397485} :answer-field/id #uuid "4ea71ffc-f171-5611-bfc4-d57724eb7008" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397482}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3d6ca311-b229-51f5-b6ee-b805697465fa" :question/math-academy-id "q-340669" :question/problem "$$\n⟨f(x),g(x)⟩={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }f(x)g(x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nGiven the inner product on the set of continuous functions over $x\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ]$ defined above, select the correct options below.$\\,$\n$⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6x),1⟩=$ \n{{field-1}}\n\n$\\,$\nThe functions $f(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6x)$ and $g(x)=1$ \n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "Evaluating the inner product, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6x),1⟩ & ={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[-\\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6x)]}_{-\\pi }^{\\pi } \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{6}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6\\pi )-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-6\\pi )) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{6}(1-1) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6x)$ and $g(x)=1$ $\\text{are orthogonal}$ with respect to the inner product."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186397487 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "532a73e6-e026-57c9-9bad-57cd2f75b6df" :knowledge-point/title "Parity Recognition and Series Type"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d6fb8e02-c2ed-54c2-a769-c0f5e618e887" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "even" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397489} {:answer/id #uuid "82f2576e-7f7d-514c-b7d3-b07f1e9d3631" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "odd" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397490}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "82f2576e-7f7d-514c-b7d3-b07f1e9d3631" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "odd" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397490} :answer-field/id #uuid "35a58589-56cd-5637-b523-03f859fac5b6" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397488} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "34d21a9d-340e-5cb4-a005-7da885a8f606" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "sine terms" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397492} {:answer/id #uuid "1b091b7e-9971-5f89-be72-b0882bfa2d62" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "cosine terms" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397493}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1b091b7e-9971-5f89-be72-b0882bfa2d62" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "cosine terms" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397493} :answer-field/id #uuid "52a06cf5-506a-5aec-8bcd-ea5d4fda8897" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397491}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8afd30f6-9418-5f9f-a8dd-bdb05d34a03e" :question/math-academy-id "q-340806" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)={x}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)$ on $x\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ].$ Select the correct options below.\n$\\,$\nThe function $f(x)$ is\n{{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$\nConsequently, the \n{{field-2}}\nin the corresponding Fourier series of $f(x)$ must be zero." :question/worked-solution "To determine whether $f$ is even or odd, we compare $f(-x)$ and $f(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-x) & =(-x)^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-x) \\\\  & =-{x}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x) \\\\  & =-f(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $f(-x)=-f(x),$ we have that $f$ is odd.\n\nFor Fourier series on $[-\\pi ,\\pi ],$ the cosine coefficients are given by\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }f(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nand the constant coefficient is\n\n$$\n{a}_{0}=\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nHere, $f(x)$ is odd and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)$ is even, so $f(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)$ is odd. Also, $f(x)$ itself is odd.\n\nNow, the integral of an odd function over $[-\\pi ,\\pi ]$ is $0.$ Thus, ${a}_{n}=0$ for all $n\\ge 1,$ and ${a}_{0}=0.$\n\nTherefore, the function $f(x)$ is $\\text{odd}$ and the $\\text{cosine terms}$ in its Fourier series must be zero."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186397494 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8ec39e02-0c39-5aa4-90e2-3477259d6262" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Laplace Transforms of Step Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6d80295e-b463-5e7c-bf1a-e7d0d9f86058" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "s>2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397496} {:answer/id #uuid "08ab9610-98a7-5832-b2b7-84ed96b7fb63" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "s>5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397497} {:answer/id #uuid "660727ef-7b2d-531b-8658-07e77f29dd8e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "s>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397498}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "660727ef-7b2d-531b-8658-07e77f29dd8e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "s>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397498} :answer-field/id #uuid "3811f53b-a1af-5f49-ab19-7ecfb660367f" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397495} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e77341d6-4bd5-508f-b10c-e6b1abd62aa6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2{e}^{-2s}}{s}-\\frac{5{e}^{-5s}}{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397500} {:answer/id #uuid "bdd85fe4-dc49-5e10-8a3c-4aae5e0e97e9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{-5s}}{s}-\\frac{{e}^{-2s}}{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397501} {:answer/id #uuid "5a60055f-03ee-5493-96d8-f946458ffa97" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{-2s}}{s}-\\frac{{e}^{-5s}}{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397502} {:answer/id #uuid "ec6b9f59-ce56-5db4-9460-84a4cfcd9c8c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2{e}^{-2s}}{s}+\\frac{5{e}^{-5s}}{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397503} {:answer/id #uuid "ab7ef87a-673e-59b2-8d9c-a72764dc0faa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{-2s}}{s}+\\frac{{e}^{-5s}}{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397504}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ab7ef87a-673e-59b2-8d9c-a72764dc0faa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{-2s}}{s}+\\frac{{e}^{-5s}}{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397504} :answer-field/id #uuid "3311ff79-3ee4-524b-acee-81bb9dd688cc" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397499}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "94c1a445-854c-59cd-8bb7-f1e99e5361c5" :question/math-academy-id "q-340883" :question/problem "Use the definition of Laplace transforms to calculate $F(s)=L{f(t)}$ for $f(t)=u(t-2)+u(t-5),$ where $u$ is the unit step function.$\\,F(s)=L{f(t)}=$ {{field-1}},\nvalid for {{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "We apply the definition of the Laplace transform:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{f(t)} & =F(s)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }f(t){e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that the unit step function is defined as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(t)={\\begin{aligned}0,\\, & t<0 \\\\ 1,\\, & t\\ge 0.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, for the piecewise functions $u(t-2)$ and $u(t-5),$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(t-2) & ={\\begin{aligned}0,\\, & t<2 \\\\ 1,\\, & t\\ge 2\\end{aligned} \\\\ u(t-5) & ={\\begin{aligned}0,\\, & t<5 \\\\ 1,\\, & t\\ge 5.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nu(t-2)+u(t-5)={\\begin{aligned}0,\\, & t<2 \\\\ 1,\\, & 2\\le t<5 \\\\ 2,\\, & t\\ge 5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, for our Laplace transform, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }f(t){e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2}^{5}0\\cdot {e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{2}^{5}1\\cdot {e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{5}^{\\infty }2\\cdot {e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2}^{5}{e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{5}^{\\infty }2{e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that the second integral diverges for $s\\le 0.$\n\nFor $s>0,$ we calculate our Laplace transform as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & ={\\int }_{2}^{5}{e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{5}^{\\infty }2{e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2}^{5}{e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t+2{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{5}^{a}{e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={[-\\frac{{e}^{-st}}{s}]}_{2}^{5}+2{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{(-\\frac{{e}^{-st}}{s})|}_{5}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{{e}^{-5s}}{s}+\\frac{{e}^{-2s}}{s}-\\frac{2}{s}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }({e}^{-sa}-{e}^{-5s}) \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{-2s}}{s}-\\frac{{e}^{-5s}}{s}-\\frac{2}{s}(0-{e}^{-5s}) \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{-2s}}{s}-\\frac{{e}^{-5s}}{s}+\\frac{2{e}^{-5s}}{s} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{-2s}}{s}+\\frac{{e}^{-5s}}{s}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Laplace transform of $f(t)$ is\n\n$$\nL{f(t)}=\\frac{{e}^{-2s}}{s}+\\frac{{e}^{-5s}}{s},\\,s>0.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222031 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2ae32a2a-438f-547c-8510-7e9f760c3cb6" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Properties of Periodic Extensions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e48d92fd-eb5d-54d5-8a5a-8eba48db736d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "True" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397506} {:answer/id #uuid "c5a91d3a-6b36-5a0c-8213-9c8a1b4a253c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "False" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397507}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e48d92fd-eb5d-54d5-8a5a-8eba48db736d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "True" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397506} :answer-field/id #uuid "aec337f7-4ecf-50fc-9a79-93502b4464a2" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397505} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "018f92a0-3544-5274-a50b-8ab59d99d148" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "True" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397509} {:answer/id #uuid "8da1c6b9-b712-5f96-ba6a-7e76dc3b56a8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "False" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397510}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "018f92a0-3544-5274-a50b-8ab59d99d148" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "True" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397509} :answer-field/id #uuid "2fdcb018-a1ac-541a-9916-1b8c916a43a1" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397508} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cf7a93f0-2cfa-5048-9f86-bb34deaaea7a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "True" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397512} {:answer/id #uuid "d7341562-4fa3-5103-b7de-023d18a018f1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "False" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397513}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cf7a93f0-2cfa-5048-9f86-bb34deaaea7a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "True" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397512} :answer-field/id #uuid "6bb56a2a-394d-574e-ad3d-34e850271acd" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397511} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fff6d535-67cf-5d92-a24c-282ed886a3f3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "True" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397515} {:answer/id #uuid "13360f60-446f-5ba5-8fe4-4db596852ca9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "False" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397516}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fff6d535-67cf-5d92-a24c-282ed886a3f3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "True" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397515} :answer-field/id #uuid "ecd037f3-14ce-5899-ac94-7cf1fd33f9d0" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397514}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2cc2bb4c-12ce-5499-9381-7f752bdaab23" :question/math-academy-id "q-341129" :question/problem "$$\nF(x)={\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}, & x\\in [0,3) \\\\ -3x, & x\\in [-3,0)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe above function $F(x)$ is defined on $x\\in [-3,3).$ Let $f(x)$ be the $6$-periodic extension of $F(x)$ to $x\\in R.$\nSelect the correct options below.\n\n| I. | $F(x)$ is continuous on $x\\in (-3,3)$ | {{field-1}} |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| II. | $F(x)$ is piecewise smooth on $x\\in (-3,3)$ | {{field-2}} |\n| III. | ${lim}_{x\\to -{3}^{+}}F(x)={lim}_{x\\to {3}^{-}}F(x)$ | {{field-3}} |\n| IV. | $f(x)$ is piecewise smooth and continuous on $R$ | {{field-4}} |" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine the given statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is $\\text{true}.$ The function $F(x)$ is continuous on $x\\in (-3,3).$ Its graph is shown below.\n\n![](images/18/186106373f9848cfefefb23197e7508497ff3d570f326da2ca342d078682128d.png)\n\n- Statement II is $\\text{true}.$ The function $F(x)$ is piecewise smooth on $x\\in (-3,3).$ It consists of two smooth pieces.\n\n- Statement III is $\\text{true}.$ Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -{3}^{+}}F(x)={lim}_{x\\to -{3}^{+}}-3x=-3(-3)=9\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {3}^{-}}F(x)={lim}_{x\\to {3}^{-}}{x}^{2}=(3)^{2}=9.\n$$\n\nSo, ${lim}_{x\\to -{3}^{+}}F(x)={lim}_{x\\to {3}^{-}}F(x).$\n\n- Statement IV is $\\text{true}$ since statements I, II, and III are all true.\n\n**Note:** The $6$-periodic extension of $F(x)$ to $x\\in R$ has the graph shown below.\n\n![](images/89/89c3a597ce1114fee9e775f291cf89e8ca04f61eb39cf2b4c896dd4be8022f09.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186397517 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c086bc97-415d-52c9-b26a-02dc8aebef33" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Laplace Transforms of Trigonometric and Hyperbolic Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a145f15c-ceb4-50f6-84e5-8b7834619199" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "s>4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397519} {:answer/id #uuid "29c9ba6e-cb4a-511a-a3e8-afa19fa0cd85" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "s\\ge 4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397520} {:answer/id #uuid "a538529b-1b93-5744-80c9-65509cce8a2b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "s>-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397521} {:answer/id #uuid "651aadd0-1e95-54cb-96ff-254e6eef8952" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "s\\ge 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397522} {:answer/id #uuid "871991e0-f1d8-558e-90c5-5f962efd70eb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "s>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397523} {:answer/id #uuid "3aaeac27-3d73-5c9c-86eb-0838799cd60f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "s\\ge -4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397524}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "871991e0-f1d8-558e-90c5-5f962efd70eb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "s>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397523} :answer-field/id #uuid "4550c540-2a81-570b-87fb-628f14326571" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397518} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8ee99e0c-d4c4-5917-b998-b7dec7f37dcd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{{s}^{2}+16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397526} {:answer/id #uuid "2bb3ed42-d158-5ad8-af7f-d31ff6b6b3c8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4s}{{s}^{2}+16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397527} {:answer/id #uuid "4a13a688-1c5e-567d-bb42-ea175ded90cf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}-16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397528} {:answer/id #uuid "7aac7682-f32b-5b3d-b4d4-32d39a154d58" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{{s}^{2}-16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397529} {:answer/id #uuid "88396452-9be4-582b-b646-fd6f295eadb8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397530}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "88396452-9be4-582b-b646-fd6f295eadb8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397530} :answer-field/id #uuid "b87144df-013d-5d61-8a79-c97d52888b76" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397525}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "db14dea0-146d-5df5-a79a-6c50bf00be55" :question/math-academy-id "q-341136" :question/problem "Use the Laplace transform of $f(t)={e}^{\\text{i}\\omega t}$ to find the Laplace transform of $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t).$$\\,L{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)}=$ {{field-1}},\ndefined for {{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "The linearity property of the Laplace transform states that for functions $f(t)$ and $g(t),$ we have\n\n$$\nL{\\alpha f(t)+\\beta g(t)}=\\alpha \\cdot L{f(t)}+\\beta \\cdot L{g(t)},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants.\n\nFirst, recall that the cosine function can be defined in terms of complex exponentials as follows: \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)=\\frac{{e}^{4\\text{i}t}+{e}^{-4\\text{i}t}}{2}\n$$\n\nTaking the Laplace transform and applying the linearity property, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)} & =L{\\frac{{e}^{4\\text{i}t}+{e}^{-4\\text{i}t}}{2}} \\\\  & =L{\\frac{{e}^{4\\text{i}t}}{2}+\\frac{{e}^{-4\\text{i}t}}{2}} \\\\  & =L{\\frac{{e}^{4\\text{i}t}}{2}}+L{\\frac{{e}^{-4\\text{i}t}}{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}L{{e}^{4\\text{i}t}}+\\frac{1}{2}L{{e}^{-4\\text{i}t}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we recall that \n\n$$\nL{{e}^{\\text{i}\\omega t}}=\\frac{1}{s-\\text{i}\\omega },\\,s>0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{e}^{4\\text{i}t}} & =\\frac{1}{s-4\\text{i}},\\,s>0 \\\\ L{{e}^{-4\\text{i}t}} & =\\frac{1}{s+4\\text{i}},\\,s>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Laplace transform of $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)} & =\\frac{1}{2}L{{e}^{4\\text{i}t}}+\\frac{1}{2}L{{e}^{-4\\text{i}t}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{s-4\\text{i}}+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{s+4\\text{i}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{1}{s-4\\text{i}}+\\frac{1}{s+4\\text{i}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{(s+4\\text{i})+(s-4\\text{i})}{(s-4\\text{i})(s+4\\text{i})}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{2s}{{s}^{2}+16}) \\\\  & =\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+16},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ndefined for $s>0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186397531 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c086bc97-415d-52c9-b26a-02dc8aebef33" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Laplace Transforms of Trigonometric and Hyperbolic Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "461b0ac0-5211-5e64-aa63-d08093e5e804" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+64}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397533} {:answer/id #uuid "a7b6b192-ec4b-5aca-a7e5-b7e2362c242a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8s}{{s}^{2}-64}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397534} {:answer/id #uuid "992044c1-c091-557f-b232-73069b3560a8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{{s}^{2}+64}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397535} {:answer/id #uuid "2e13c630-4b5b-5b09-ab98-ae7663e336b9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}-64}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397536} {:answer/id #uuid "e26f2812-efee-58e1-bc49-fa54b5fa6464" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{{s}^{2}-64}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397537}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e26f2812-efee-58e1-bc49-fa54b5fa6464" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{{s}^{2}-64}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397537} :answer-field/id #uuid "4e065cee-e495-59be-a49b-48b465530e20" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397532} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "758b98a0-94c0-5be6-b097-b85d31f91123" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "s\\ge 8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397539} {:answer/id #uuid "91240e34-abfd-5826-835b-a95cbbd93c8a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "s>8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397540} {:answer/id #uuid "c1c6fc60-0e73-5a81-8093-d5c8cfec9a2a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "s\\ge -8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397541} {:answer/id #uuid "93d7be6d-9a7a-59df-b1e3-a5deb3df60a9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "s>-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397542} {:answer/id #uuid "3942e53b-efa0-5e2e-aeb2-41186bd71234" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "s\\ge 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397543} {:answer/id #uuid "9f65ca28-dfe3-5a9c-8051-5e8c9de202af" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "s>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397544}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "91240e34-abfd-5826-835b-a95cbbd93c8a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "s>8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397540} :answer-field/id #uuid "e8d76374-c302-5673-ab25-1a0eb5568341" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397538}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f89dc302-60ce-5bd9-b567-660590ce563f" :question/math-academy-id "q-341140" :question/problem "Use the Laplace transform of $f(t)={e}^{kt}$ to find the Laplace transform of $\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(8t).$$\\,L{\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(8t)}=$ {{field-1}},\ndefined for {{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "The linearity property of the Laplace transform states that for functions $f(t)$ and $g(t),$ we have\n\n$$\nL{\\alpha f(t)+\\beta g(t)}=\\alpha \\cdot L{f(t)}+\\beta \\cdot L{g(t)},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants.\n\nFirst, recall that, by definition\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(8t)=\\frac{{e}^{8t}-{e}^{-8t}}{2}.\n$$\n\nTaking the Laplace transform and applying the linearity property, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(8t)} & =L{\\frac{{e}^{8t}-{e}^{-8t}}{2}} \\\\  & =L{\\frac{{e}^{8t}}{2}-\\frac{{e}^{-8t}}{2}} \\\\  & =L{\\frac{{e}^{8t}}{2}}-L{\\frac{{e}^{-8t}}{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}L{{e}^{8t}}-\\frac{1}{2}L{{e}^{-8t}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we recall that \n\n$$\nL{{e}^{kt}}=\\frac{1}{s-k},\\,s>k.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{e}^{8t}} & =\\frac{1}{s-8},\\,s>8 \\\\ L{{e}^{-8t}} & =\\frac{1}{s+8},\\,s>-8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Laplace transform of $\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(8t)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(8t)} & =\\frac{1}{2}L{{e}^{8t}}-\\frac{1}{2}L{{e}^{-8t}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{s-8}-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{s+8} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{1}{s-8}-\\frac{1}{s+8}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{(s+8)-(s-8)}{(s-8)(s+8)}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{16}{{s}^{2}-64}) \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{{s}^{2}-64}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $L{{e}^{8t}}$ is defined for $s\\in (8,\\infty )$ and $L{{e}^{-8t}}$ is defined for $s\\in (-8,\\infty ),$ we have that $L{\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(8t)}$ is defined for\n\n$$\n(8,\\infty )\\cap (-8,\\infty )=(8,\\infty ),\n$$\n\nthat is, $s>8.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186397545 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "304c015d-cbb6-5989-a102-d6f0f5f38604" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Expressions For Fourier Coefficients"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d7d31a3-b2e8-5955-b7a5-8d0265e34185" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(3x+1)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397547} {:answer/id #uuid "14f752d4-26f3-5b55-951a-267514a31272" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }(3x+1)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397548} {:answer/id #uuid "86ecc11d-d361-56e3-b985-f0af7df41e5b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }(3x+1)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397549} {:answer/id #uuid "3ecabd1c-c6be-5ae6-a083-97bdd6237c82" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(3x+1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397550} {:answer/id #uuid "b23326b9-dc50-5189-864f-580f0beb25f2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(3x+1)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397551}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b23326b9-dc50-5189-864f-580f0beb25f2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(3x+1)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397551} :answer-field/id #uuid "40e8826c-c720-5c71-8e60-9efc9028ee9f" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397546} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "74b0c5c0-5b9c-5ca0-a3b8-c0e23762591f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397553} {:answer/id #uuid "037567f7-c39a-5a21-9477-5930025ede7c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397554} {:answer/id #uuid "5a2e6cae-f02e-5650-9bdf-362d1f1fa2b1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397555} {:answer/id #uuid "64699d60-b695-59b3-84f5-62b93bdee4e2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397556} {:answer/id #uuid "4c59d8f0-ecad-5bf2-9285-f977292bfdcf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397557}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4c59d8f0-ecad-5bf2-9285-f977292bfdcf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397557} :answer-field/id #uuid "df14591b-b6cc-5cdd-adc7-9dbfe95edc2a" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397552} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cd7a6256-c073-5d27-93a0-6184aec661c2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{⟨f(x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)⟩}{⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)⟩}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397559} {:answer/id #uuid "e3d09424-4779-58cb-a9a8-a20b503a58f9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{⟨f(x),1⟩}{⟨1,1⟩}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397560} {:answer/id #uuid "fd0b285c-99a5-5dc6-b53e-349ca170a4bd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2⟨f(x),1⟩}{⟨1,1⟩}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397561} {:answer/id #uuid "d6de7f97-87a8-587c-be9e-52648a5b0102" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{⟨f(x),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)⟩}{⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)⟩}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397562} {:answer/id #uuid "330f62f3-2b9c-5888-9cec-d52701d82d24" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{⟨1,1⟩}{⟨f(x),1⟩}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397563}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fd0b285c-99a5-5dc6-b53e-349ca170a4bd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2⟨f(x),1⟩}{⟨1,1⟩}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397561} :answer-field/id #uuid "7e635cb7-5fb0-5dc2-9345-111ede6d51d4" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397558}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "73b220ad-78ed-5227-b775-ed2065f7b0fd" :question/math-academy-id "q-341308" :question/problem "Let $F(x)=3x+1$ for $x\\in R,$ and let $f(x)$ be the $2\\pi $-periodic extension of $F$ from $x\\in [0,2\\pi )$ to $x\\in R.$ The Fourier series of $f$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }({a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)).\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options in the expression for the coefficient ${a}_{0}$ below, where $⟨\\,,⟩$ denotes the usual inner product on functions over $[0,2\\pi ).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{0} & ={{field-1}} \\\\  & ={{field-2}}\\,\\cdot \\,{{field-3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The usual inner product of functions $f$ and $g$ over $x\\in [0,2\\pi )$ is given by\n\n$$\n⟨f(x),g(x)⟩={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }f(x)g(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nFor a $2\\pi $-periodic function $f(x)$ that's piecewise continuous over $x\\in [0,2\\pi ),$ the Fourier series of $f$ is given by \n\n$$\nf(x)∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }({a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)),\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{0},$ ${a}_{n},$ and ${b}_{n}$ are constants to be determined. \n\nNow, we find the inner product of $f(x)$ with $1:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨f(x),1⟩ & = \\\\ ⟨\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }({a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)),1⟩ & = \\\\ ⟨\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2},1⟩+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }⟨{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx),1⟩+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }⟨{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx),1⟩ & = \\\\ \\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}⟨1,1⟩+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx),1⟩+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx),1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the functions\n$1,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x),…$\nare orthogonal with respect to the defined inner product, the only nonzero term in the final expression above is\n\n$$\n\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}⟨1,1⟩.\n$$\n\nComputing the inner product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨1,1⟩ & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }1\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[x]}_{-0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =2\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨f(x),1⟩ & =\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}⟨1,1⟩ \\\\ {a}_{0} & =\\frac{2⟨f(x),1⟩}{⟨1,1⟩} \\\\ {a}_{0} & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(3x+1)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186397564 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "424fc261-d1ca-5bf0-b085-7af1de6cc7cc" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Laplace Transforms of Other Piecewise Continuous Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "04fdc9fb-cd44-5e0e-b22f-8f67fce82a51" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "s>3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397566} {:answer/id #uuid "cabe113f-508f-5a0e-ae94-fc0083b16c13" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "s\\ge 3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397567} {:answer/id #uuid "0cc99fcc-e772-5e94-b870-35a9db5a4ba3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "s\\ge 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397568} {:answer/id #uuid "a6ae2c3f-920d-5b6e-8987-c30c8599133a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "s\\ge 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397569} {:answer/id #uuid "d4a6dad1-27fa-519f-81f0-cc70bb51b3f5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "s>1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397570} {:answer/id #uuid "99f28136-fb56-52a1-98a2-8be91b7068aa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "s>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397571}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d4a6dad1-27fa-519f-81f0-cc70bb51b3f5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "s>1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397570} :answer-field/id #uuid "a55cae0b-d0e7-5c92-b5d3-5cd9673f9f43" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397565} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "61ae7223-aa34-5db5-aa66-4e5fde9de0ed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{s}-\\frac{2{e}^{-3s}}{s}+\\frac{{e}^{3(s-1)}}{s-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397573} {:answer/id #uuid "b0648719-f6f1-5582-a9c7-b2e40208d75c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{s}+\\frac{{e}^{-3s}}{s}+\\frac{{e}^{3(1-s)}}{s-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397574} {:answer/id #uuid "ce80b731-5ad8-572d-9659-a7d36ed14cbd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{s}-\\frac{2{e}^{-3s}}{s}+\\frac{{e}^{3(1-s)}}{s-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397575} {:answer/id #uuid "edaa60e1-e666-514b-893d-b9aec909db8b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{s}-\\frac{2{e}^{-3s}}{s}+\\frac{{e}^{3(1-s)}}{s+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397576}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ce80b731-5ad8-572d-9659-a7d36ed14cbd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{s}-\\frac{2{e}^{-3s}}{s}+\\frac{{e}^{3(1-s)}}{s-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397575} :answer-field/id #uuid "87e3c7a6-fbb4-5070-994b-44c52d38d82f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397572}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d8e97793-b7f7-5bb2-8257-c5344e5ddb06" :question/math-academy-id "q-341448" :question/problem "Use the definition of Laplace transforms to calculate $F(s)=L{f(t)},$ where $f(t)$ is given by \n\n$$\nf(t)={\\begin{aligned}2,\\, & t\\le 3 \\\\ {e}^{t}\\, & t>3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$\\,F(s)=L{f(t)}=$ {{field-1}},\nvalid for {{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "We apply the definition of the Laplace transform:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{f(t)} & =F(s)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }f(t){e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, for the piecewise function \n\n$$\nf(t)={\\begin{aligned}2,\\, & t\\le 3 \\\\ {e}^{t}\\, & t>3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }f(t){e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}2\\cdot {e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{3}^{\\infty }{e}^{t}\\cdot {e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}2\\cdot {e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{3}^{\\infty }{e}^{t-st}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}2{e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{3}^{\\infty }{e}^{(1-s)t}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that the improper integral diverges if $s\\le 1.$\n\nFor $s>1,$ we calculate our Laplace transform as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}2{e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{3}^{\\infty }{e}^{(1-s)t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{s}{[{e}^{-st}]}_{0}^{3}+{\\int }_{3}^{\\infty }{e}^{(1-s)t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{s}-\\frac{2{e}^{-3s}}{s}+{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{3}^{a}{e}^{(1-s)t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{s}-\\frac{2{e}^{-3s}}{s}+{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{(\\frac{{e}^{(1-s)t}}{1-s})|}_{3}^{a} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{s}-\\frac{2{e}^{-3s}}{s}+\\frac{1}{1-s}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }({e}^{(1-s)a}-{e}^{3(1-s)}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{s}-\\frac{2{e}^{-3s}}{s}+\\frac{1}{1-s}\\cdot (0-{e}^{3(1-s)}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{s}-\\frac{2{e}^{-3s}}{s}+\\frac{{e}^{3(1-s)}}{s-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nvalid for $s>1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186396660 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5985dfc1-a05b-5ac9-a33d-8398478e252c" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Piecewise-Continuous Functions Over Infinite Intervals"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "70a234cb-554b-545b-99a9-38ca1d4a7d30" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$h$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396662} {:answer/id #uuid "813b9b1c-2e23-599a-bbed-341899317794" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f,g,$ and $h$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396663} {:answer/id #uuid "43827749-fd32-5ade-b947-0ae8c476e27e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$g$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396664} {:answer/id #uuid "4c4fa032-2148-54c2-9c32-cbe3a90719b0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$g$ and $h$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396665} {:answer/id #uuid "53e9c955-42a6-5020-a355-eae4fe66d4c8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f$ and $h$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396666}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "813b9b1c-2e23-599a-bbed-341899317794" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f,g,$ and $h$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396663} :answer-field/id #uuid "7aedcafb-9859-554f-b742-1fe94afe482b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396661}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4689ffaf-d7a6-5ea0-9161-3ec67ff19338" :question/math-academy-id "q-341642" :question/problem "Which of the following functions are piecewise continuous on $x\\in [0,\\infty ).$\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\sqrt{x+1},\\,g(x)={\\begin{aligned}1,\\, & ⌈x⌉\\text{ is even} \\\\ -1,\\, & ⌈x⌉\\text{ is odd},\\end{aligned}\\,h(x)=u(x-1)+u\\,(x-\\frac{5}{2})+u(x-4).\n$$\n\nNote that $u(x)$ is the unit step function, and $⌈x⌉$ is the ceiling function." :question/worked-solution "Suppose $I⊆R.$ A function $f:I\\to R$ is piecewise continuous on $I$ if it is piecewise continuous on every closed and bounded subinterval $[a,b]⊂I.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each function.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is piecewise continuous over $x\\in [0,\\infty ).$ The function is continuous over $x\\in [0,\\infty ).$\n\n- The function $g(x)$ is piecewise continuous over $x\\in [0,\\infty ).$ The function has jump discontinuities at $x=0,1,2,…,$ and any closed interval $[a,b]⊂[0,\\infty )$ will contain finitely many discontinuities.\n\n![](images/d5/d5a497337426dad07096702a3e211f525a959add8cb1fbaaef05fccac26d634b.png)\n\n- The function $h(x)$ is piecewise continuous over $x\\in [0,\\infty )$ since it is continuous over $x\\in [0,\\infty )$ except at the points $x=1,$ $x=\\frac{5}{2},$ and $x=4,$ where the one-sided limits exist and are finite:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}h(x)=0,\\,{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}h(x)=1\n$$\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 5/{2}^{-}}h(x)=1,\\,{lim}_{x\\to 5/{2}^{+}}h(x)=2\n$$\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {4}^{-}}h(x)=2,\\,{lim}_{x\\to {4}^{+}}h(x)=3\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$f,g,$ and $h.$\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124867 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "71f8b1c9-7856-52fa-8990-a83fda9ef2a9" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying Points for a Differential Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "42d4e07a-aa53-5b14-88a3-04309d900d01" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "an irregular singular" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397578} {:answer/id #uuid "80394f18-b738-54ac-9c5e-5b5f1497c047" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "an ordinary" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397579} {:answer/id #uuid "2c3b1e62-fd12-53e6-a2e6-437965ed144c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "a regular singular" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397580}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "80394f18-b738-54ac-9c5e-5b5f1497c047" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "an ordinary" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397579} :answer-field/id #uuid "4b0b6010-a8e4-5818-9b9e-07b9fa2156c5" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397577} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bbed6fca-b1b4-5cb5-a628-05c2a2876f25" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "an irregular singular" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397582} {:answer/id #uuid "f6c04487-b3e1-54a8-91d4-f475965ddee1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "an ordinary" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397583} {:answer/id #uuid "d23321d7-6d21-5e82-b71a-8df7426c4beb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "a regular singular" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397584}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bbed6fca-b1b4-5cb5-a628-05c2a2876f25" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "an irregular singular" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397582} :answer-field/id #uuid "27f98080-0f8c-5167-a519-b69c9b9da579" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397581} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "121b342d-94a4-5a32-b807-9fd677a437eb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "an irregular singular" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397586} {:answer/id #uuid "006f2dab-a7ef-56db-8b52-1e8b65fff7ca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "an ordinary" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397587} {:answer/id #uuid "deca4897-4c3e-5bad-9f24-155edaba92f8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "a regular singular" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397588}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "deca4897-4c3e-5bad-9f24-155edaba92f8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "a regular singular" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397588} :answer-field/id #uuid "b18cd1f9-4635-565b-9f81-413f7fa8c5bf" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397585} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ae1974be-cae5-5a5c-b85c-da80cb6ca3f0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "an irregular singular" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397590} {:answer/id #uuid "a8e73a36-ba65-5180-ab1b-3d21c5dd5547" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "an ordinary" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397591} {:answer/id #uuid "ec690560-1681-5c8e-9a5e-d9729f1a5cb8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "a regular singular" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397592}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ae1974be-cae5-5a5c-b85c-da80cb6ca3f0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "an irregular singular" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397590} :answer-field/id #uuid "c4dc4c18-d716-57a7-86b8-c7cc062be0ae" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397589}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9c2710b8-4c49-5de2-8583-5f8c80fe5c91" :question/math-academy-id "q-342410" :question/problem "$$\nx(x-3)(x+1){y}^{″}+(x-3){y}^{′}+x(x+1)(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)y=0\n$$\n\nClassify the following points for the differential equation given above.$\\,$The point $x=-1$ is\n{{field-1}}\npoint of the differential equation.\n\n$\\,$The point $x=0$ is\n{{field-2}}\npoint of the differential equation.\n\n$\\,$The point $x=2$ is\n{{field-3}}\npoint of the differential equation.\n\n$\\,$The point $x=3$ is\n{{field-4}}\npoint of the differential equation." :question/worked-solution "The point $x=a$ is an ordinary point of the differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+P(x){y}^{′}+Q(x)y=f(x)\n$$\n\nif both $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ are analytic about $x=a.$ Otherwise, $x=a$ is a singular point of the differential equation.\n\nA singular point $x=a$ is regular if both $(x-a)P(x)$ and $(x-a)^{2}Q(x)$ are analytic about $x=a.$ Otherwise, the singular point is irregular.\n\nFirst, we rewrite the differential equation in standard form by dividing by $x(x-3)(x+1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(x-3)(x+1){y}^{″}+(x-3){y}^{′}+x(x+1)(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)y & =0 \\\\ {y}^{″}+\\frac{1}{x(x+1)}{y}^{′}+\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{x-3}y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nP(x)=\\frac{1}{x(x+1)}\\,\\text{and}\\,Q(x)=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{x-3}.\n$$\n\nThe function $P(x)$ is *not* analytic about points where $x(x+1)=0,$ which occurs at $x=0$ and $x=-1.$\n\nThe function $Q(x)$ is *not* analytic about points where $x-3=0$ or where $\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ is undefined. These occur at $x=3$ and $x\\le 0.$\n\nTherefore, $x=2$ is $\\text{an ordinary}$ point of the differential equation, and the singular points are\n\n$$\nx=-1,\\,x=0,\\,x=3.\n$$\n\nNow, let's classify each singular point.\n\n- For $x=-1,$ the function\n\n$$\n(x+1)P(x)=(x+1)\\cdot \\frac{1}{x(x+1)}=\\frac{1}{x},\n$$\n\nis analytic about $x=-1,$ and the function\n\n$$\n(x+1)^{2}Q(x)=(x-1)^{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{x-3},\n$$\n\nis undefined about $x=-1.$ So, $x=-1$ is $\\text{an irregular singular}$ point of the differential equation.\n\n- For $x=0,$ the function\n\n$$\nxP(x)=x\\cdot \\frac{1}{x(x+1)}=\\frac{1}{x+1},\n$$\n\nis analytic about $x=0,$ but the function\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}Q(x)={x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{x-3},\n$$\n\nis undefined about $x=0.$ So, $x=0$ is $\\text{an irregular singular}$ point of the differential equation.\n\n- For $x=3,$ the function\n\n$$\n(x-3)P(x)=(x-3)\\cdot \\frac{1}{x(x+1)},\n$$\n\nis analytic about $x=3,$ and the function\n\n$$\n(x-3)^{2}Q(x)=(x-3)^{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{x-3}=(x-3)\\operatorname{ln}⁡x,\n$$\n\nis also analytic about $x=3.$ Therefore, $x=3$ is $\\text{a regular singular}$ point of the differential equation."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186397593 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2ae32a2a-438f-547c-8510-7e9f760c3cb6" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Properties of Periodic Extensions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8755164a-0677-56b8-a14a-88ef64412325" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "True" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397595} {:answer/id #uuid "fd07ffa2-060b-5390-8c1e-f3fa7f05b91e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "False" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397596}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fd07ffa2-060b-5390-8c1e-f3fa7f05b91e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "False" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397596} :answer-field/id #uuid "f2cb40da-a7f4-5c99-8c06-a63a258898b3" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397594} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f672220a-3d44-5a57-9dc4-f393d21fd036" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "True" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397598} {:answer/id #uuid "778d4e9c-9ed6-508e-89cf-bb2d161c14a6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "False" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397599}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "778d4e9c-9ed6-508e-89cf-bb2d161c14a6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "False" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397599} :answer-field/id #uuid "37025846-4756-5f39-9aec-cf0c7c1f7476" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397597} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "96826b32-74fa-517b-814f-48bf8e8d6576" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "True" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397601} {:answer/id #uuid "f2ba36c9-f2ba-565a-b1c4-51749cec3189" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "False" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397602}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f2ba36c9-f2ba-565a-b1c4-51749cec3189" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "False" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397602} :answer-field/id #uuid "c95a34df-dd3f-5841-9258-36788f5137b0" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397600} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3a784da2-d4e4-5d4b-b900-c6ed1e90fed0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "True" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397604} {:answer/id #uuid "4b07bc9b-8163-5605-b7bf-f62facb32b99" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "False" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397605}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4b07bc9b-8163-5605-b7bf-f62facb32b99" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "False" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397605} :answer-field/id #uuid "9b046f21-736c-5f03-a92f-dec58bf868ea" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397603}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "24b6f004-4c8b-5565-a4ef-1d172930ca34" :question/math-academy-id "q-342657" :question/problem "$$\nF(x)={\\begin{aligned}x+2, & x\\in [-2,0] \\\\ \\frac{1}{x}, & x\\in (0,2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe above function $F(x)$ is defined on $x\\in [-2,2).$ Let $f(x)$ be the $4$-periodic extension of $F(x)$ to $x\\in R.$\nSelect the correct options below.\n\n| I. | $F(x)$ is continuous on $x\\in (-2,2)$ | {{field-1}} |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| II. | $F(x)$ is piecewise smooth on $x\\in (-2,2)$ | {{field-2}} |\n| III. | ${lim}_{x\\to -{2}^{+}}F(x)={lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}F(x)$ | {{field-3}} |\n| IV. | $f(x)$ is piecewise smooth and continuous on $R$ | {{field-4}} |" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine the given statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is $\\text{false}.$ The function $F(x)$ is not continuous on $x\\in (-2,2)$ because $\\frac{1}{x}\\to \\infty $ as $x\\to {0}^{+}.$ Its graph is shown below.\n\n![](images/8e/8e5eec10427a7061a05c0bd1228ce1146234bf2b09d75bb86ba83b28fbe178d9.png)\n\n- Statement II is $\\text{false}.$ The function $F(x)$ is not piecewise smooth on $x\\in (-2,2)$ because ${F}^{′}(x)=-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\to -\\infty $ as $x\\to {0}^{+}.$\n\n- Statement III is $\\text{false}.$ Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -{2}^{+}}F(x)={lim}_{x\\to -{2}^{+}}(x+2)=-2+2=0\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}F(x)={lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}\\frac{1}{x}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nSo, ${lim}_{x\\to -{2}^{+}}F(x)\\ne {lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}F(x).$\n\n- Statement IV is $\\text{false}$ since statements I and II are false.\n\n**Note:** The $4$-periodic extension of $F(x)$ to $x\\in R$ has the graph shown below.\n\n![](images/4a/4afcdf86f777b9c7afc8af31b36cde4b80cfe394fd908db1ccd47ef48dff1617.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186397606 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "06fedec8-5aed-52f9-bd4f-aa81b9180ad4" :knowledge-point/title "Solving First-Order Initial Value Problems With Sinusoidal Forcing"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "488da8c0-0995-59d5-a5c6-54b8e67769d2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{s-4}{(s+2)({s}^{2}+16)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397608} {:answer/id #uuid "a55e62b5-e47d-5336-b8b7-276e9c1dbb68" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{s+4}{(s+2)({s}^{2}+16)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397609} {:answer/id #uuid "f47e74e2-4bce-5a44-817d-59bd1c489c8c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{(s+2)({s}^{2}+16)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397610} {:answer/id #uuid "02cd391d-9944-5308-bbce-ea584369dba1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{s}{(s+2)({s}^{2}+16)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397611} {:answer/id #uuid "79c67eea-4e83-584e-9efc-0ea95542dae5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4-s}{(s+2)({s}^{2}+16)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397612}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "79c67eea-4e83-584e-9efc-0ea95542dae5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4-s}{(s+2)({s}^{2}+16)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397612} :answer-field/id #uuid "2c237e54-7764-534a-9c54-8baed4a37d20" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397607} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2225ade3-5bf4-52e8-bb8f-84401c8e6e34" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{s+2}-\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397614} {:answer/id #uuid "9a61bdd8-3d2f-572c-91f5-833d4e189e88" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{10(s+2)}-\\frac{3s+4}{10({s}^{2}+16)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397615} {:answer/id #uuid "4d73190f-40e0-562b-8571-039c42b83caf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{s+2}-\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397616} {:answer/id #uuid "d6087e3e-f080-5ae6-bb69-2acbb87ff902" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{10(s+2)}+\\frac{3s+4}{10({s}^{2}+16)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397617}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9a61bdd8-3d2f-572c-91f5-833d4e189e88" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{10(s+2)}-\\frac{3s+4}{10({s}^{2}+16)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397615} :answer-field/id #uuid "e8f001e5-aaed-5c9c-a1ac-1f1aa18ef7db" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397613} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "24c20f35-2312-5896-8f00-c76ef490da78" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{10}{e}^{-2t}+\\frac{3}{10}\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t-\\frac{1}{10}\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397619} {:answer/id #uuid "287b1162-26e2-52bb-99aa-815c269f399d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-2t}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397620} {:answer/id #uuid "a3aed227-d2f9-59e8-8313-76d8cc49a807" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{10}{e}^{-2t}-\\frac{3}{10}\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t-\\frac{1}{10}\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397621} {:answer/id #uuid "7e56b7cb-60e3-5844-8de1-228ec8842538" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{10}{e}^{-2t}-\\frac{3}{10}\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t+\\frac{1}{10}\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397622}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a3aed227-d2f9-59e8-8313-76d8cc49a807" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{10}{e}^{-2t}-\\frac{3}{10}\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t-\\frac{1}{10}\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397621} :answer-field/id #uuid "6e35ac8d-bb69-5204-8ed0-7880d0ca6330" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397618}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1873e66d-b721-525d-8ac0-7b13c3d92c58" :question/math-academy-id "q-342960" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem.\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}+2y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t,\\,y(0)=0\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options in the reasoning below to find the solution using Laplace transforms. You may make use of the following results:\n\n$$\nL{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t}=\\frac{\\omega }{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}},\\,L{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t}=\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}},\\,s>0.\n$$\n\nLet $Y(s)=L{y(t)}.$ Taking the Laplace transform of both sides of the equation and solving for $Y,$ we have\n\n$$\nY(s)={{field-1}}.\n$$\n\nThe partial fraction decomposition of $Y$ is\n\n$$\nY(s)={{field-2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny(t)={{field-3}}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we take the Laplace transform of both sides of the differential equation. Using the linearity property, we have\n\n$$\nL{{y}^{′}}+2L{y}=L{\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t}-L{\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t}.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula for the transform of a derivative $L{{y}^{′}}=sY(s)-y(0)$ and the standard trigonometric transforms\n\n$$\nL{\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t}=\\frac{4}{{s}^{2}+16},\\,L{\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t}=\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+16},\n$$\n\nwe substitute the known values. Note that $y(0)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(sY(s)-0)+2Y(s) & =\\frac{4}{{s}^{2}+16}-\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+16} \\\\ (s+2)Y(s) & =\\frac{4-s}{{s}^{2}+16}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe rearrange to solve for $Y(s).$ Dividing by $(s+2),$ we obtain\n\n$$\nY(s)=\\frac{4-s}{(s+2)({s}^{2}+16)}.\n$$\n\nNext, we perform a partial fraction decomposition. Since the denominator has a linear factor $(s+2)$ and an irreducible quadratic factor $({s}^{2}+16),$ we write\n\n$$\n\\frac{4-s}{(s+2)({s}^{2}+16)}=\\frac{A}{s+2}+\\frac{Bs+C}{{s}^{2}+16}.\n$$\n\nTo find the constants, we clear the denominators:\n\n$$\n4-s=A({s}^{2}+16)+(Bs+C)(s+2)\n$$\n\n- Setting $s=-2,$ we have\n \n\n$$\n4-(-2)=A((-2)^{2}+16)+0\\,⟹\\,6=20A\\,⟹\\,A=\\frac{3}{10}.\n$$\n\n- Comparing the coefficients of ${s}^{2},$ we have\n  \n\n$$\n0=A+B\\,⟹\\,0=\\frac{3}{10}+B\\,⟹\\,B=-\\frac{3}{10}.\n$$\n\n- Comparing the constant terms, we have\n \n\n$$\n4=16A+2C\\,⟹\\,4=\\frac{48}{10}+2C\\,⟹\\,C=-\\frac{2}{5}.\n$$\n\nThus, the decomposition is\n\n$$\nY(s)=\\frac{3}{10(s+2)}+\\frac{(-\\frac{3}{10}s-\\frac{2}{5})}{{s}^{2}+16}=\\frac{3}{10(s+2)}-\\frac{3s+4}{10({s}^{2}+16)}.\n$$\n\nFrom the table of Laplace transforms, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{L}^{-1}{\\frac{1}{s+2}} & ={e}^{-2t} \\\\ {L}^{-1}{\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+16}} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t \\\\ {L}^{-1}{\\frac{4}{{s}^{2}+16}} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, taking the inverse Laplace transform of $Y(s),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(t) & =\\frac{3}{10}{L}^{-1}{\\frac{1}{s+2}}-\\frac{3}{10}{L}^{-1}{\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+16}}-\\frac{1}{10}{L}^{-1}{\\frac{4}{{s}^{2}+16}} \\\\ y(t) & =\\frac{3}{10}{e}^{-2t}-\\frac{3}{10}\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t-\\frac{1}{10}\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186397623 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "734fe467-57d6-5b3f-a61e-1e2e4624b5fc" :knowledge-point/title "Using Abel's Identity to Conclude Independence/Dependence"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "304d5117-ee46-5c5d-afa7-ca920f029e8f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$W(x)\\ne 0$ for all $x\\in R$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397625} {:answer/id #uuid "ae08826c-6c1c-5987-ad7e-864c4360f574" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$W(x)=0$ for all $x\\in R$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397626} {:answer/id #uuid "b83dbcc8-f9de-571f-b680-6fc8b19ba9d4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$W(x)=0$ for some but not all $x\\in R$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397627}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "304d5117-ee46-5c5d-afa7-ca920f029e8f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$W(x)\\ne 0$ for all $x\\in R$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397625} :answer-field/id #uuid "622cd65e-d7fb-587b-8c10-1defb970706b" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397624} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7ef66bf4-a759-5faa-bbe6-c8479e7aca1e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4Q(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397629} {:answer/id #uuid "07f88880-7512-5cb1-85f0-9acaa96748b6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397630} {:answer/id #uuid "ecd7fc4f-0bb8-5d15-9606-f2346f18bdfc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397631} {:answer/id #uuid "52543647-4678-5589-9673-644a893cea31" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4Q(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397632}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ecd7fc4f-0bb8-5d15-9606-f2346f18bdfc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397631} :answer-field/id #uuid "80ef2068-6b95-504c-8d87-420eff7181f4" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397628} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9e8310ce-fbd3-5538-b766-45d5514898c8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "C{x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397634} {:answer/id #uuid "e082f656-48a6-5575-b482-8353f60d9a9d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Cx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397635} {:answer/id #uuid "1d0a044d-7c2c-5bb4-bfbf-18edf83e4c37" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "C{e}^{-4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397636} {:answer/id #uuid "b96d58ae-2f44-580e-81fa-71567a86ef0b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "C{e}^{4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397637}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b96d58ae-2f44-580e-81fa-71567a86ef0b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "C{e}^{4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397637} :answer-field/id #uuid "70866db8-d47c-5d65-8fab-fa76cb30f2a9" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397633}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "65f6d9f7-7688-5460-b686-2b9904fe578a" :question/math-academy-id "q-343289" :question/problem "$$\n{y}^{″}-4{y}^{′}+Q(x)y=0\n$$\n\nLet ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ be solutions to the second-order homogeneous differential equation above, where $Q(x)$ is continuous on $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ The Wronskian satisfies $W(0)=-1.$ Select the correct options in the reasoning below.The Wronskian $W(x)$ satisfies the following first-order differential equation:\n\n$$\n{W}^{′}(x)={{field-1}}W(x)\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we get\n\n$$\nW={{field-2}}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nNow, using the fact that $W(0)=-1,$ we obtain \n{{field-3}}." :question/worked-solution "Abel's identity says that if ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions of\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+P(x){y}^{′}+Q(x)y=0,\n$$\n\nthen their Wronskian $W(x)$ satisfies\n\n$$\n{W}^{′}(x)=-P(x)W(x).\n$$\n\nIn our equation, the coefficient of ${y}^{′}$ is \n\n$$\nP(x)=-4.\n$$\n\nSubstituting into Abel's identity gives\n\n$$\n{W}^{′}(x)=4W(x).\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the first-order differential equation for $W:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{W}^{′} & =4W \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}W}{W} & =4\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{\\text{d}W}{W} & =\\int 4\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|W| & =4x+K \\\\ |W| & ={e}^{K}{e}^{4x} \\\\ W & =C{e}^{4x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant.\n\nNext, we use the given value $W(0)=-1$ to find $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W(0) & =C{e}^{4\\cdot 0} \\\\ -1 & =C\\cdot 1 \\\\ C & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have $W(x)=-{e}^{4x}$ and $W(x)\\ne 0\\text{ for all }x\\in R.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186397638 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "734fe467-57d6-5b3f-a61e-1e2e4624b5fc" :knowledge-point/title "Using Abel's Identity to Conclude Independence/Dependence"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d90121c9-177f-56cb-abef-4c63bdbede7d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "C{e}^{{x}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397640} {:answer/id #uuid "513bee4c-d7a9-5b9d-8a90-fb020c324ef7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{C}{{e}^{x}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397641} {:answer/id #uuid "912232e2-b616-5b37-bd1b-5a8f05973914" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{C}{{x}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397642} {:answer/id #uuid "17aa114d-1675-5b0a-92ea-b1131bf9c02e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "C{e}^{-{x}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397643}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "17aa114d-1675-5b0a-92ea-b1131bf9c02e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "C{e}^{-{x}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397643} :answer-field/id #uuid "12e79a8a-77ce-5206-ad24-806b35c154fb" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397639} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9f5325b4-2c7f-510a-836c-8d30b25fc79f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$W(x)=0$ for some but not all $x>0$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397645} {:answer/id #uuid "f7a00866-c222-5cf7-8082-6ea525338ab3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$W(x)\\ne 0$ for all $x>0$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397646} {:answer/id #uuid "2c1bd3d6-1ec9-5357-b88e-6e77f2e5915e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$W(x)=0$ for all $x>0$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397647}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2c1bd3d6-1ec9-5357-b88e-6e77f2e5915e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$W(x)=0$ for all $x>0$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397647} :answer-field/id #uuid "9ab8824e-5b62-51f7-bcc4-ce3c21af44e9" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397644} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fac610d4-aa9e-59b8-b9b1-3082677c09d1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2x}{Q(x)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397649} {:answer/id #uuid "aea7672b-d42a-5cb9-abad-b947e31b87bf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397650} {:answer/id #uuid "f9a49da1-ba65-5fd3-9e45-8c74fb4bd158" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2xQ(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397651} {:answer/id #uuid "86e3e6c4-6ee0-562c-b132-7062dfe19170" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397652}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "aea7672b-d42a-5cb9-abad-b947e31b87bf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397650} :answer-field/id #uuid "d476b0bc-73ec-5b5c-aed8-1c9dde581b2e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397648}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4acb3708-3f85-57a1-975e-b6f23d95dbae" :question/math-academy-id "q-343293" :question/problem "$$\n{y}^{″}+2x{y}^{′}+Q(x)y=0,\\,x>0\n$$\n\nLet ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ be solutions to the second-order homogeneous differential equation above, where $Q(x)$ is continuous on $(0,\\infty ).$ The Wronskian satisfies $W(2)=0.$ Select the correct options in the reasoning below.\nThe Wronskian $W(x)$ satisfies the following first-order differential equation:\n\n$$\n{W}^{′}(x)={{field-1}}W(x)\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we get\n\n$$\nW={{field-2}}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nNow, using the fact that $W(2)=0,$ we obtain \n{{field-3}}." :question/worked-solution "Abel's identity says that if ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions of\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+P(x){y}^{′}+Q(x)y=0,\n$$\n\nthen their Wronskian $W(x)$ satisfies\n\n$$\n{W}^{′}(x)=-P(x)W(x).\n$$\n\nIn our equation, the coefficient of ${y}^{′}$ is \n\n$$\nP(x)=2x.\n$$\n\nSubstituting into Abel's identity gives\n\n$$\n{W}^{′}(x)=-2xW(x).\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the first-order differential equation for $W:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{W}^{′} & =-2xW \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}W}{W} & =-2x\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{\\text{d}W}{W} & =\\int -2x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|W| & =-{x}^{2}+K \\\\ |W| & ={e}^{K}{e}^{-{x}^{2}} \\\\ W & =C{e}^{-{x}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant.\n\nNext, we use the given value $W(2)=0$ to find $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W(2) & =C{e}^{-{2}^{2}} \\\\ 0 & =C{e}^{-4} \\\\ C & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have $W(x)=0\\text{ for all }x>0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186397653 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "734fe467-57d6-5b3f-a61e-1e2e4624b5fc" :knowledge-point/title "Using Abel's Identity to Conclude Independence/Dependence"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "de6a57db-718b-534a-ae9e-131e468efa23" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3Q(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397655} {:answer/id #uuid "a9ce1615-d523-520a-92c9-77d8376540ed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3Q(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397656} {:answer/id #uuid "7849e7e8-dd36-565f-b3df-32eaf5d73858" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397657} {:answer/id #uuid "6418d95c-2071-5910-935c-316e5a6e14e4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397658}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6418d95c-2071-5910-935c-316e5a6e14e4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397658} :answer-field/id #uuid "2abe0e81-32ea-5c8a-8474-a8da7cef4208" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397654} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ced5090f-a9f5-5a32-868e-dc53d28d58ee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "C{e}^{3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397660} {:answer/id #uuid "774462d4-46f9-512e-8905-d39831ad658e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "C{e}^{-3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397661} {:answer/id #uuid "2575b061-d8fc-52a6-b1af-7e66bd3daf8d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "C{x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397662} {:answer/id #uuid "2ade0a28-db59-5ae1-84d7-b56b3670e989" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Cx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397663}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "774462d4-46f9-512e-8905-d39831ad658e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "C{e}^{-3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397661} :answer-field/id #uuid "2812c0aa-a8f5-52e5-9ddb-2cd3402c3d64" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397659} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7ded8315-1dbf-5143-8c09-39b574a39a0a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$W(x)\\ne 0$ for all $x\\in R$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397665} {:answer/id #uuid "703b696a-f747-5010-96b9-919bbf602029" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$W(x)=0$ for all $x\\in R$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397666} {:answer/id #uuid "a35cefdf-5d10-56e7-a5d1-a38747aa53bf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$W(x)=0$ for some but not all $x\\in R$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397667}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "703b696a-f747-5010-96b9-919bbf602029" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$W(x)=0$ for all $x\\in R$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397666} :answer-field/id #uuid "02065a3e-a8fd-5833-9e30-70ce022d3cfe" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397664}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0b1f9810-dc22-5af5-9afc-b7fe4c62290d" :question/math-academy-id "q-343295" :question/problem "$$\n{y}^{″}+3{y}^{′}+Q(x)y=0\n$$\n\nLet ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ be solutions to the second-order homogeneous differential equation above, where $Q(x)$ is continuous on $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ The Wronskian satisfies $W(0)=0.$ Select the correct options in the reasoning below.The Wronskian $W(x)$ satisfies the following first-order differential equation:\n\n$$\n{W}^{′}(x)={{field-1}}W(x)\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we get\n\n$$\nW={{field-2}}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nNow, using the fact that $W(0)=0,$ we obtain \n{{field-3}}." :question/worked-solution "Abel's identity says that if ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions of\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+P(x){y}^{′}+Q(x)y=0,\n$$\n\nthen their Wronskian $W(x)$ satisfies\n\n$$\n{W}^{′}(x)=-P(x)W(x).\n$$\n\nIn our equation, the coefficient of ${y}^{′}$ is \n\n$$\nP(x)=3.\n$$\n\nSubstituting into Abel's identity gives\n\n$$\n{W}^{′}(x)=-3W(x).\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the first-order differential equation for $W:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{W}^{′} & =-3W \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}W}{W} & =-3\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{\\text{d}W}{W} & =\\int -3\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|W| & =-3x+K \\\\ |W| & ={e}^{K}{e}^{-3x} \\\\ W & =C{e}^{-3x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant.\n\nNext, we use the given value $W(0)=0$ to find $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W(0) & =C{e}^{-3\\cdot 0} \\\\ 0 & =C\\cdot 1 \\\\ C & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have $W(x)=0\\text{ for all }x\\in R.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186397668 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4733fab7-18ca-5336-9aba-53212ddbf87b" :knowledge-point/title "Solving First Order ODEs Given the Fourier Series of the Forcing Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "af7b840f-2e0c-52c0-b79f-c78277457040" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{54}{n\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+81)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397670} {:answer/id #uuid "addf1d04-69f0-5452-bba4-8900e12fa907" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{162}{n\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+81)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397671} {:answer/id #uuid "8626a439-85ec-5b4a-9bb1-b4485f902170" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{162}{n\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+81)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397672}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8626a439-85ec-5b4a-9bb1-b4485f902170" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{162}{n\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+81)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397672} :answer-field/id #uuid "865ba785-fbd6-5a07-957c-16cb2c1df477" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397669} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1a53b81f-d126-598c-8b37-6e225ea3cb17" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{6}{n({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+81)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397674} {:answer/id #uuid "f5abb3ad-0dba-5b32-98f6-df1e57e1d85e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+81}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397675} {:answer/id #uuid "0102c81a-3dd5-541b-9257-ca77f5322bb8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{18}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+81}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397676}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0102c81a-3dd5-541b-9257-ca77f5322bb8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{18}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+81}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397676} :answer-field/id #uuid "91f55656-129c-5fe5-8079-32fba851d88e" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397673} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "22623b70-5aab-52fc-9402-941db3c703fc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397678} {:answer/id #uuid "ee445dc1-2657-5591-8683-e95785717f43" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397679} {:answer/id #uuid "d51d9118-2abe-5ef4-9ada-c8a08d3b5d43" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397680} {:answer/id #uuid "d01d3c08-0c36-57bc-8823-3c469f701c68" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397681}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "22623b70-5aab-52fc-9402-941db3c703fc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397678} :answer-field/id #uuid "4d625df2-64a2-5d5b-b5dc-aa82f78979a2" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397677}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "15f5a0b9-1297-5188-a047-103209518fed" :question/math-academy-id "q-343855" :question/problem "Select the missing parts in a particular solution of the differential equation ${y}^{′}-3y=f(x),$ where the Fourier series of the $6$-periodic forcing function is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)∼-2+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{6}{n\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}).\n$$\n\nThe Fourier series of a particular solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}∼{{field-1}} & +{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,{{field-2}}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}) \\\\  & +{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,{{field-3}}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We assume that a solution of our equation has the following Fourier series:\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}).\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & ∼\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})) \\\\  & ∼0+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\cdot (-\\frac{n\\pi }{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}))+{b}_{n}\\cdot \\frac{n\\pi }{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}) \\\\  & ∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }-\\frac{{a}_{n}n\\pi }{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})+\\frac{{b}_{n}n\\pi }{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the expressions for ${y}_{p}^{′}$ and ${y}_{p}$ into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & ({\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }-\\frac{{a}_{n}n\\pi }{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})+\\frac{{b}_{n}n\\pi }{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})) \\\\  & \\,-3(\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})) \\\\  & \\,\\,=-2+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{6}{n\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRearranging the terms, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & -3\\cdot \\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }(\\frac{{b}_{n}n\\pi }{3}-3{a}_{n})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}) \\\\  & \\,\\,+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }(-\\frac{{a}_{n}n\\pi }{3}-3{b}_{n})\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}) \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,=-2+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{6}{n\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, equating the free terms and the coefficients next to sines and cosines, we obtain the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-3\\cdot \\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}=-2 \\\\ \\frac{{b}_{n}n\\pi }{3}-3{a}_{n}=0 \\\\ -\\frac{{a}_{n}n\\pi }{3}-3{b}_{n}=\\frac{6}{n\\pi }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation, ${a}_{0}=\\frac{4}{3},$ which means the constant term is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}=\\frac{2}{3}.\n$$\n\nNext, from the second equation, ${b}_{n}=\\frac{9{a}_{n}}{n\\pi }.$ Substituting this into the third equation, gives\n\n$$\n-\\frac{{a}_{n}n\\pi }{3}-3(\\frac{9{a}_{n}}{n\\pi })=\\frac{6}{n\\pi }\\,⇒\\,{a}_{n}=-\\frac{18}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+81}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, for ${a}_{n}$ with $n\\ge 1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{b}_{n} & =\\frac{9{a}_{n}}{n\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{n\\pi }\\cdot (-\\frac{18}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+81}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{162}{n\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+81)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our particular solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}∼\\frac{2}{3} & +{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,-\\frac{18}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+81}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}) \\\\  & +{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,-\\frac{162}{n\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+81)}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186397682 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4733fab7-18ca-5336-9aba-53212ddbf87b" :knowledge-point/title "Solving First Order ODEs Given the Fourier Series of the Forcing Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f5beaf63-a24e-5096-a60c-b0469d5c3b91" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397684} {:answer/id #uuid "abc5583a-c2d4-5270-9535-e8dc76611c8d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397685} {:answer/id #uuid "20b43c86-9df5-5ea5-b8db-701f1d2fcb5a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397686} {:answer/id #uuid "f8b221ad-c466-515a-9758-208b0b79820b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397687}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "abc5583a-c2d4-5270-9535-e8dc76611c8d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397685} :answer-field/id #uuid "dfa212b9-4a9e-5ed1-977d-8a0f92f187bb" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397683} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c55a33cd-7217-5f5d-aae1-44a553c062e0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+225}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397689} {:answer/id #uuid "68fd5497-22fd-5e95-baa6-a3fb2a651897" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{30(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+225}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397690} {:answer/id #uuid "9261cac5-a1b2-5b9e-85af-f8f060b21c0e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{6(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+225}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397691}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c55a33cd-7217-5f5d-aae1-44a553c062e0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+225}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397689} :answer-field/id #uuid "685da459-566a-5cb9-907e-c163e95f44e3" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397688} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "52840280-13ca-54da-b4d4-afd74b42c883" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{18(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+225)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397693} {:answer/id #uuid "7120f89a-088f-5258-8c2d-358cc96f6e1c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{90(-1)^{n}}{n\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+225)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397694} {:answer/id #uuid "15b8dddf-399a-5ce8-a3a7-717ad28083fc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{90(-1)^{n}}{n\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+225)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397695}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7120f89a-088f-5258-8c2d-358cc96f6e1c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{90(-1)^{n}}{n\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+225)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397694} :answer-field/id #uuid "615c3feb-87ab-595d-bd3c-9d3dd60c0bff" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397692}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b2c46dfd-7116-546d-b775-da0e3f310626" :question/math-academy-id "q-343860" :question/problem "Select the missing parts in a particular solution of the differential equation ${y}^{′}-5y=f(x),$ where the Fourier series of the $6$-periodic forcing function is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)∼-5+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{2(-1)^{n+1}}{n\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}).\n$$\n\nThe Fourier series of a particular solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}∼{{field-1}} & +{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,{{field-2}}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}) \\\\  & +{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,{{field-3}}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We assume that a solution of our equation has the following Fourier series:\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}).\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & ∼\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})) \\\\  & ∼0+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\cdot (-\\frac{n\\pi }{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}))+{b}_{n}\\cdot \\frac{n\\pi }{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}) \\\\  & ∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }-\\frac{{a}_{n}n\\pi }{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})+\\frac{{b}_{n}n\\pi }{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the expressions for ${y}_{p}^{′}$ and ${y}_{p}$ into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & ({\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }-\\frac{{a}_{n}n\\pi }{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})+\\frac{{b}_{n}n\\pi }{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})) \\\\  & \\,-5(\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})) \\\\  & \\,\\,=-5+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{2(-1)^{n+1}}{n\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRearranging the terms, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & -5\\cdot \\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }(\\frac{{b}_{n}n\\pi }{3}-5{a}_{n})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}) \\\\  & \\,\\,+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }(-\\frac{{a}_{n}n\\pi }{3}-5{b}_{n})\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}) \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,=-5+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{2(-1)^{n+1}}{n\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, equating the free terms and the coefficients next to sines and cosines, we obtain the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-5\\cdot \\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}=-5 \\\\ \\frac{{b}_{n}n\\pi }{3}-5{a}_{n}=0 \\\\ -\\frac{{a}_{n}n\\pi }{3}-5{b}_{n}=\\frac{2(-1)^{n+1}}{n\\pi }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation, ${a}_{0}=2,$ which means the constant term is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}=1.\n$$\n\nNext, from the second equation, ${b}_{n}=\\frac{15{a}_{n}}{n\\pi }.$ Substituting this into the third equation, gives\n\n$$\n-\\frac{{a}_{n}n\\pi }{3}-5(\\frac{15{a}_{n}}{n\\pi })=\\frac{2(-1)^{n+1}}{n\\pi }\\,⇒\\,{a}_{n}=\\frac{6(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+225}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, for ${a}_{n}$ with $n\\ge 1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{b}_{n} & =\\frac{15{a}_{n}}{n\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{15}{n\\pi }\\cdot \\frac{6(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+225} \\\\  & =\\frac{90(-1)^{n}}{n\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+225)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our particular solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}∼1 & +{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,\\frac{6(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+225}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}) \\\\  & +{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,\\frac{90(-1)^{n}}{n\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+225)}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186397696 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9e42e65b-6e26-5a3f-9275-25a291e13138" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Resonant Harmonics"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "26abab5a-e0d3-5a63-b7b9-8c8b067c042b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "n=4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397698} {:answer/id #uuid "1bc27d3d-2228-5745-9f75-6ad665b3203a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "n=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397699} {:answer/id #uuid "c26489de-65c7-593c-9bc1-121a32b98a82" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "n=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397700} {:answer/id #uuid "efa4633c-d9b6-5177-bd10-dd0e9bfa3eb3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "n=3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397701}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "efa4633c-d9b6-5177-bd10-dd0e9bfa3eb3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "n=3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397701} :answer-field/id #uuid "9d3c5e72-c342-555b-a87c-e14e208988df" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397697} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bc221971-12de-52c7-8478-0068ddd7a170" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x}{54\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6\\pi x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397703} {:answer/id #uuid "fe05ab27-ffe9-519c-a245-1e3105109d19" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{x}{108\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6\\pi x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397704} {:answer/id #uuid "96ad6016-80ff-5081-b817-514fd1499c89" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x}{108\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6\\pi x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397705} {:answer/id #uuid "0e910e33-e3b2-5731-a6d5-5a16e1e9ad14" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x}{216\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6\\pi x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397706}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "96ad6016-80ff-5081-b817-514fd1499c89" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x}{108\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6\\pi x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397705} :answer-field/id #uuid "9fa9bba7-0c1d-559d-9dd6-0cd8ed6cec73" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397702}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d694cb78-fd17-5d72-bd5e-fb91875a20a6" :question/math-academy-id "q-343891" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation ${y}^{″}+36{\\pi }^{2}y=f(x),$ where the $1$-periodic forcing function is given by \n\n$$\nf(x)∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{n}^{2}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2n\\pi x).\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options in the reasoning below regarding the resonant component of a particular solution of the equation.\n\nThe natural frequency of the system matches the frequency of the {{field-1}} harmonic of the forcing function. \n\nConsequently, the particular solution contains a resonant term of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{{p}_{3}}={{field-2}}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We begin by identifying resonance. The differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+36{\\pi }^{2}y=f(x)\n$$\n\nhas a natural frequency of \n\n$$\n\\omega =\\sqrt{36{\\pi }^{2}}=6\\pi .\n$$\n\nThe frequencies of the forcing harmonics for a $1$-periodic function $(L=\\frac{1}{2})$ are \n\n$$\n{\\omega }_{n}=\\frac{n\\pi }{(\\frac{1}{2})}=2n\\pi .\n$$\n\nFor $n=3$, we have ${\\omega }_{3}=6\\pi .$ Since ${\\omega }_{3}=\\omega ,$ the $n=3$ harmonic of the forcing function causes resonance.\n\nFor the $n=3$ harmonic, the forcing term is \n\n$$\n{f}_{3}(x)=\\frac{1}{9}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6\\pi x).\n$$\n\nBecause this frequency matches the natural frequency, we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{{p}_{3}}=x(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6\\pi x)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6\\pi x)).\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{{p}_{3}}^{′} & =(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6\\pi x)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6\\pi x))+x(-6\\pi A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6\\pi x)+6\\pi B\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6\\pi x)) \\\\ {y}_{{p}_{3}}^{″} & =-12\\pi A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6\\pi x)+12\\pi B\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6\\pi x)-36{\\pi }^{2}x(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6\\pi x)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6\\pi x))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the expressions for ${y}_{{p}_{3}}^{″}$ and ${y}_{{p}_{3}}$ into the equation ${y}^{″}+36{\\pi }^{2}y=\\frac{1}{9}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6\\pi x):$\n\n$$\n[-12\\pi A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6\\pi x)+12\\pi B\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6\\pi x)-36{\\pi }^{2}{y}_{{p}_{3}}]+36{\\pi }^{2}{y}_{{p}_{3}}=\\frac{1}{9}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6\\pi x)\n$$\n\nThe resonant terms involving $x$ cancel out, leaving\n\n$$\n-12\\pi A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6\\pi x)+12\\pi B\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6\\pi x)=\\frac{1}{9}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6\\pi x).\n$$\n\nNow, equating the sine and cosine coefficients, we obtain:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-12\\pi A=0 \\\\ 12\\pi B=\\frac{1}{9}\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,{\\begin{aligned}A=0 \\\\ B=\\frac{1}{108\\pi }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the resonant component of a particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{{p}_{3}}=\\frac{x}{108\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6\\pi x).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186397707 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9e42e65b-6e26-5a3f-9275-25a291e13138" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Resonant Harmonics"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "60cf0d89-49e8-5b67-80c1-e7d2d7a3ffb4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{x}{8\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397709} {:answer/id #uuid "a1876296-7211-55be-aba4-bfdcc1a01df6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x}{16\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397710} {:answer/id #uuid "09aff7b4-dbdd-58ab-8229-b25ef4b4ba18" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x}{4\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397711} {:answer/id #uuid "fe23c02e-5cbf-556d-b7f3-55b796e71bfc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x}{8\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397712}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fe23c02e-5cbf-556d-b7f3-55b796e71bfc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x}{8\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397712} :answer-field/id #uuid "ef63660a-274c-5dd7-9740-8afb5e0d6094" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397708} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1b222e16-92bf-5e7b-a075-99b8093081a2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "n=4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397714} {:answer/id #uuid "e3cd2371-beae-59d9-8d7e-94aae5b22c28" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "n=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397715} {:answer/id #uuid "964c305f-0261-5625-959d-d4db38c740ee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "n=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397716} {:answer/id #uuid "63032ea2-0b98-59b3-8f8b-f8122510525d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "n=3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397717}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "964c305f-0261-5625-959d-d4db38c740ee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "n=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397716} :answer-field/id #uuid "9f3873a4-d6a5-52d5-8df1-9961c00d5836" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397713}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "dd94ef93-5daf-566f-92be-7aef938749ca" :question/math-academy-id "q-343899" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation ${y}^{″}+{\\pi }^{2}y=f(x),$ where the $4$-periodic forcing function is given by \n\n$$\nf(x)∼5+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{n}^{2}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}).\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options in the reasoning below regarding the resonant component of a particular solution of the equation.\n\nThe natural frequency of the system matches the frequency of the {{field-1}} harmonic of the forcing function. \n\nConsequently, the particular solution contains a resonant term of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{{p}_{2}}={{field-2}}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We begin by identifying resonance. The differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+{\\pi }^{2}y=f(x)\n$$\n\nhas a natural frequency of \n\n$$\n\\omega =\\sqrt{{\\pi }^{2}}=\\pi .\n$$\n\nThe frequencies of the forcing harmonics for a $4$-periodic function $(L=2)$ are \n\n$$\n{\\omega }_{n}=\\frac{n\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nFor $n=2$, we have ${\\omega }_{2}=\\pi .$ Since ${\\omega }_{2}=\\omega ,$ the $n=2$ harmonic of the forcing function causes resonance.\n\nFor the $n=2$ harmonic, the forcing term is \n\n$$\n{f}_{2}(x)=\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x).\n$$\n\nBecause this frequency matches the natural frequency, we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{{p}_{2}}=x(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x)).\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{{p}_{2}}^{′} & =(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x))+x(-\\pi A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x)+\\pi B\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x)) \\\\ {y}_{{p}_{2}}^{″} & =-2\\pi A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x)+2\\pi B\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x)-{\\pi }^{2}x(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the expressions for ${y}_{{p}_{2}}^{″}$ and ${y}_{{p}_{2}}$ into the equation ${y}^{″}+{\\pi }^{2}y=\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x):$\n\n$$\n[-2\\pi A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x)+2\\pi B\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x)-{\\pi }^{2}{y}_{{p}_{2}}]+{\\pi }^{2}{y}_{{p}_{2}}=\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x)\n$$\n\nThe resonant terms involving $x$ cancel out, leaving\n\n$$\n-2\\pi A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x)+2\\pi B\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x)=\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x).\n$$\n\nNow, equating the sine and cosine coefficients, we obtain:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-2\\pi A=0 \\\\ 2\\pi B=\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,{\\begin{aligned}A=0 \\\\ B=\\frac{1}{8\\pi }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the resonant component of a particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{{p}_{2}}=\\frac{x}{8\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186397718 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9e42e65b-6e26-5a3f-9275-25a291e13138" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Resonant Harmonics"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "718e9566-c7c9-5a9a-b62c-206f45871768" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "n=4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397720} {:answer/id #uuid "4123d653-3eb6-56f8-9a54-2ef04840a633" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "n=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397721} {:answer/id #uuid "5c2e6e0a-4098-5507-86d3-f9eae8b3dec8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "n=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397722} {:answer/id #uuid "d77b424d-712f-5538-af48-9fa7a0208031" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "n=3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397723}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d77b424d-712f-5538-af48-9fa7a0208031" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "n=3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397723} :answer-field/id #uuid "6c917125-cff6-58cf-bd8e-53fff9587ed6" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397719} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d4a9dd1f-56de-5787-b2b5-60a35b059c5c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x}{27\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\pi x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397725} {:answer/id #uuid "216bc2a3-5817-55ad-92ea-6ce57b3a3498" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{x}{54\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\pi x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397726} {:answer/id #uuid "17e5708c-ca06-5b4e-bd8a-6d23c5265ddf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{x}{27\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\pi x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397727} {:answer/id #uuid "fd5c93f6-1ed7-54a8-a40e-3a422b0a0168" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{x}{9\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\pi x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397728}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "17e5708c-ca06-5b4e-bd8a-6d23c5265ddf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{x}{27\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\pi x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397727} :answer-field/id #uuid "69b53d84-a0ad-59e0-b26e-795d6cf7c7fe" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397724}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "68ceb45a-31c0-520d-b3cb-f81e2d91b31a" :question/math-academy-id "q-343910" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation ${y}^{″}+9{\\pi }^{2}y=f(x),$ where the $2$-periodic forcing function is given by \n\n$$\nf(x)∼1+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{2}{{n}^{2}}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi x).\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options in the reasoning below regarding the resonant component of a particular solution of the equation.\n\nThe natural frequency of the system matches the frequency of the {{field-1}} harmonic of the forcing function. \n\nConsequently, the particular solution contains a resonant term of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{{p}_{3}}={{field-2}}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We begin by identifying resonance. The differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+9{\\pi }^{2}y=f(x)\n$$\n\nhas a natural frequency of \n\n$$\n\\omega =\\sqrt{9{\\pi }^{2}}=3\\pi .\n$$\n\nThe frequencies of the forcing harmonics for a $2$-periodic function $(L=1)$ are \n\n$$\n{\\omega }_{n}=\\frac{n\\pi }{1}=n\\pi .\n$$\n\nFor $n=3$, we have ${\\omega }_{3}=3\\pi .$ Since ${\\omega }_{3}=\\omega ,$ the $n=3$ harmonic of the forcing function causes resonance.\n\nFor the $n=3$ harmonic, the forcing term is \n\n$$\n{f}_{3}(x)=\\frac{2}{9}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\pi x).\n$$\n\nBecause this frequency matches the natural frequency, we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{{p}_{3}}=x(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\pi x)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\pi x)).\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{{p}_{3}}^{′} & =(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\pi x)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\pi x))+x(-3\\pi A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\pi x)+3\\pi B\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\pi x)) \\\\ {y}_{{p}_{3}}^{″} & =-6\\pi A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\pi x)+6\\pi B\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\pi x)-9{\\pi }^{2}x(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\pi x)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\pi x))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the expressions for ${y}_{{p}_{3}}^{″}$ and ${y}_{{p}_{3}}$ into the equation ${y}^{″}+9{\\pi }^{2}y=\\frac{2}{9}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\pi x):$\n\n$$\n[-6\\pi A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\pi x)+6\\pi B\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\pi x)-9{\\pi }^{2}{y}_{{p}_{3}}]+9{\\pi }^{2}{y}_{{p}_{3}}=\\frac{2}{9}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\pi x)\n$$\n\nThe resonant terms involving $x$ cancel out, leaving\n\n$$\n-6\\pi A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\pi x)+6\\pi B\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\pi x)=\\frac{2}{9}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\pi x).\n$$\n\nNow, equating the sine and cosine coefficients, we obtain:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-6\\pi A=\\frac{2}{9} \\\\ 6\\pi B=0\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,{\\begin{aligned}A=-\\frac{1}{27\\pi } \\\\ B=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the resonant component of a particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{{p}_{3}}=-\\frac{x}{27\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\pi x).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186397729 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9f542453-9758-55e6-93c6-6bfd2139ec45" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Tangent Line Approximation and Classifying it as an Underestimate or Overestimate"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4d40eb58-2655-5469-940e-f80c613a366a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397731} {:answer/id #uuid "cd504e90-e5b7-56c6-964b-40f89c7b3493" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397732} {:answer/id #uuid "a07cbcd9-56d0-586c-aa21-1a80b6af5ef6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397733} {:answer/id #uuid "9fc20b50-1f58-592c-8612-6b49865b0664" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397734} {:answer/id #uuid "286353c9-a32e-56eb-8992-b6c84200e329" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397735}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a07cbcd9-56d0-586c-aa21-1a80b6af5ef6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397733} :answer-field/id #uuid "8a9687f0-3dbc-5f03-bc8c-36c768da7028" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397730} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "92b8f43d-f4d5-509d-8b12-68825b47ce0d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "overestimate" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397737} {:answer/id #uuid "ef65b10b-e65d-5550-b964-4477632fe18b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "underestimate" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397738}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "92b8f43d-f4d5-509d-8b12-68825b47ce0d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "overestimate" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397737} :answer-field/id #uuid "52b792d3-0847-501a-907c-2ebe956b9ca4" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397736}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "fb9ed8ae-2017-567d-8d5e-a0f446be0601" :question/math-academy-id "q-346720" :question/problem "The graph of the function $y=f(x)$ passes through $(0,2)$ and is concave down on the interval $(-2,2).$ If ${f}^{′}(0)=0.5,$ estimate the value of $f(0.4)$ using the tangent line approximation of $f(x)$ at $x=0$ and determine whether the approximation is an underestimate or overestimate.$\\,f(0.4)≈$ {{field-1}} and the approximation is an {{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "The tangent line approximation of $f(x)$ at the point $a=0$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(x) & =f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a) \\\\  & =f(0)+{f}^{′}(0)(x-0).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe graph of the function passes through the point $(0,2),$ so we have $f(0)=2.$ Also, we're told that ${f}^{′}(0)=0.5.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(x) & =2+0.5x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can approximate $f(0.4)$ by evaluating the tangent line approximation at $x=0.4.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0.4) & ≈L(0.4) \\\\  & =2+0.5(0.4) \\\\  & =2.2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since the function is concave down on the interval $(-2,2),$ the linear approximation $L(x)$ is above the graph near $x=0,$ and therefore we have an $\\text{overestimate}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186397739 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7ac34a18-053d-5eab-8c2c-bd0f15658186" :knowledge-point/title "Solving First-Order Linear ODEs Using Variation of Parameters"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8f63dcef-6e20-5403-a462-b25bca77f0df" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{e}^{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397741} {:answer/id #uuid "73133d27-3932-5ef7-a71f-df7f08b40324" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397742} {:answer/id #uuid "a5984a2b-a6e3-5dd3-95eb-041759b9f99e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{e}^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397743} {:answer/id #uuid "d1e2e547-0e12-580f-ae06-681f5cc502ed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397744} {:answer/id #uuid "1f4522a5-e869-5e67-add6-a59161b129cf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{e}^{-x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397745}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1f4522a5-e869-5e67-add6-a59161b129cf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{e}^{-x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397745} :answer-field/id #uuid "69acc76f-e6dd-53c9-a2d7-4929026fb4c8" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397740} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ea685ad9-8dcf-55b7-a739-bd4306d3c8e4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397747} {:answer/id #uuid "7aab73a2-9bd4-53e3-9b2e-4069fdbada5c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{e}^{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397748} {:answer/id #uuid "8c168341-7f63-5ed7-bd61-ac7d0c8ae880" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397749} {:answer/id #uuid "513dc7fc-70e9-5782-87d8-c29a8bbc9061" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{e}^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397750} {:answer/id #uuid "84134dc2-eddb-5150-b0d9-6e42375be127" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397751}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7aab73a2-9bd4-53e3-9b2e-4069fdbada5c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{e}^{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397748} :answer-field/id #uuid "9e11331f-f12c-56ec-abc2-41fb74d0907d" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397746} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3c7b312d-000a-53d2-b64a-ae56b169c7e1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "u(x){e}^{-2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397753} {:answer/id #uuid "1236b2ee-c79e-5b48-af53-571e33ebaf43" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "u(x){e}^{3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397754} {:answer/id #uuid "bfb5d257-83c4-5b1d-bab7-bb3092c22daa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "u(x){e}^{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397755} {:answer/id #uuid "d3eaaabd-442d-53ac-81ea-8a2a5fd0cca1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "u(x){e}^{-3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397756} {:answer/id #uuid "e84d8a1c-818b-5f53-a0a3-e2ce9dab7827" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "u(x){e}^{-x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397757}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1236b2ee-c79e-5b48-af53-571e33ebaf43" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "u(x){e}^{3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397754} :answer-field/id #uuid "3394c0e8-a1fd-5164-b5fe-2278c6eec3bf" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397752}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "efe05efa-2761-5181-aebf-f550bba3f8de" :question/math-academy-id "q-352890" :question/problem "Consider the first-order linear ODE\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-3y={e}^{2x}.\n$$\n\nWe wish to solve this equation using the method of variation of parameters (VOP).\n\nSelect the correct options below.\n\n$\\,$ According to VOP, we assume a particular solution of the form {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ Solving for $u,$ we have $u(x)=$ {{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Therefore, the particular solution is ${y}_{p}(x)=$ {{field-3}}." :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}-3{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{3x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nThe method of variation of parameters constructs a particular solution by varying the constant in the homogeneous solution. So, to find a suitable ${y}_{p},$ we set $A=u(x)$ in the complementary solution, as given by the following ansatz:\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=u(x){e}^{3x}\n$$\n\nIntuitively, this form of ${y}_{p}$ tracks the homogeneous solution but with a varying amplitude.\n\nTo find $u,$ we substitute our ansatz into the equation, apply the product rule to the derivative term, and simplify to isolate ${u}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(u{e}^{3x})}^{′}-3(u{e}^{3x}) & ={e}^{2x} \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{3x}+3u{e}^{3x}-3u{e}^{3x} & ={e}^{2x} \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{3x}+\\cancel{3u{e}^{3x}}-\\cancel{3u{e}^{3x}} & ={e}^{2x} \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{3x} & ={e}^{2x} \\\\ {u}^{′} & ={e}^{-x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides with respect to $x$ (omitting the arbitrary constant), we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′} & ={e}^{-x} \\\\ \\int {u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {e}^{-x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ u & =-{e}^{-x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${y}_{p}=u(x){e}^{3x},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p} & =u(x){e}^{3x} \\\\  & =-{e}^{-x}\\cdot {e}^{3x} \\\\  & =-{e}^{2x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186397758 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7ac34a18-053d-5eab-8c2c-bd0f15658186" :knowledge-point/title "Solving First-Order Linear ODEs Using Variation of Parameters"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "36dc406b-5149-5aeb-99f4-c422c56adc09" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{3x}\\int {e}^{-3x}(1-4x)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397760} {:answer/id #uuid "11154d93-5ccc-5c68-a857-aee315edeb48" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{3x}\\int {e}^{3x}(1-4x)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397761} {:answer/id #uuid "23e73190-9abd-5014-8776-a3e439817b2e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-3x}\\int {e}^{-3x}(1-4x)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397762} {:answer/id #uuid "2db97ceb-c880-55bd-9dad-60e6eed828e7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-3x}\\int {e}^{3x}(1-4x)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397763}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2db97ceb-c880-55bd-9dad-60e6eed828e7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-3x}\\int {e}^{3x}(1-4x)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397763} :answer-field/id #uuid "5a467a6f-a5c6-5790-ac4d-f97c7812ce6a" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397759} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "10c182f9-383c-58da-882f-395c04dd6b90" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int {e}^{-3x}(1-4x)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397765} {:answer/id #uuid "a1e5a89f-4546-53e1-a022-5e7739f83b1f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int {e}^{3x}(1-4x)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397766} {:answer/id #uuid "1ec37a94-cd94-5e1f-98c5-54bc9d2c9cff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int {e}^{3x}(4x-1)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397767} {:answer/id #uuid "dd232a56-f4cc-56fc-b884-30cf0430eb44" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int {e}^{3x}(1+4x)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397768}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a1e5a89f-4546-53e1-a022-5e7739f83b1f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int {e}^{3x}(1-4x)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397766} :answer-field/id #uuid "4fff85ae-4624-593f-9e66-64f227957388" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397764} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0512b396-796a-5a66-9e01-233e25cac296" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "u(x){e}^{3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397770} {:answer/id #uuid "a5e91fdf-1722-5c86-aba4-e7001a74c175" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "u(x){e}^{4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397771} {:answer/id #uuid "81b3d8a7-3cff-52cb-8e4e-a47b128cf79e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "u(x){e}^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397772} {:answer/id #uuid "37921492-3c46-559e-816c-ff8ee78351ee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "u(x){e}^{-3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397773} {:answer/id #uuid "e2ff85d2-c6bc-5e83-bcd4-df22d2bc5e11" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "u(x){e}^{-4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397774}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "37921492-3c46-559e-816c-ff8ee78351ee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "u(x){e}^{-3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397773} :answer-field/id #uuid "48b6e071-99a9-577f-a558-4259b093492e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397769}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d21173e6-c5c3-5364-8e5b-d645bc7f05bc" :question/math-academy-id "q-352895" :question/problem "Consider the first-order linear ODE\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+3y=1-4x.\n$$\n\nWe wish to solve this equation using the method of variation of parameters (VOP).\n\nSelect the correct options below.\n\n$\\,$ According to VOP, we assume a particular solution of the form {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ Solving for $u,$ we have $u(x)=$ {{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Therefore, the particular solution is ${y}_{p}(x)=$ {{field-3}}." :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}+3{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{-3x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nThe method of variation of parameters constructs a particular solution by varying the constant in the homogeneous solution. So, to find a suitable ${y}_{p},$ we set $A=u(x)$ in the complementary solution, as given by the following ansatz:\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=u(x){e}^{-3x}\n$$\n\nIntuitively, this form of ${y}_{p}$ tracks the homogeneous solution but with a varying amplitude.\n\nTo find $u,$ we substitute our ansatz into the equation, apply the product rule to the derivative term, and simplify to isolate ${u}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(u{e}^{-3x})}^{′}+3(u{e}^{-3x}) & =1-4x \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{-3x}-3u{e}^{-3x}+3u{e}^{-3x} & =1-4x \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{-3x}-\\cancel{3u{e}^{-3x}}+\\cancel{3u{e}^{-3x}} & =1-4x \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{-3x} & =1-4x \\\\ {u}^{′} & ={e}^{3x}(1-4x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides with respect to $x$ (omitting the arbitrary constant), we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′} & ={e}^{3x}(1-4x) \\\\ \\int {u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {e}^{3x}(1-4x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ u & =\\int {e}^{3x}(1-4x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${y}_{p}=u(x){e}^{-3x},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p} & =u(x){e}^{-3x} \\\\  & =\\int {e}^{3x}(1-4x)\\,\\text{d}x\\cdot {e}^{-3x} \\\\  & ={e}^{-3x}\\int {e}^{3x}(1-4x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186397775 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "126ffd6c-f5a9-564f-935f-398a126d2cbd" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing a First-Order Linear Equation From a d'Alembert Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7ed958e4-1f96-524f-b3de-110ff91c56eb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{t}^{2}}{{t}^{2}-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397777} {:answer/id #uuid "2965ac18-e5e8-50d6-a1d8-51c095517414" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{t({t}^{2}-1)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397778} {:answer/id #uuid "1716108b-afba-5b8e-8f20-72faa3deabf0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{t}{{t}^{2}-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397779} {:answer/id #uuid "352ae16b-e108-578f-a46d-867b64df0c48" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397780}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1716108b-afba-5b8e-8f20-72faa3deabf0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{t}{{t}^{2}-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397779} :answer-field/id #uuid "66f88564-b8cd-591d-a785-13a83fdfb6b9" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397776} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "46c4364f-4fa5-52be-b64d-080c7d0e12da" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{t({t}^{2}-1)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397782} {:answer/id #uuid "52293ede-63c0-5c50-a1e8-a410e4858c4e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}({t}^{2}-1)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397783} {:answer/id #uuid "e32c59ab-db42-5218-85ae-4fb5278cdf4d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{t}{{t}^{2}-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397784} {:answer/id #uuid "7790bcee-04c3-594b-bb1b-33e4a4b043f7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397785}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "46c4364f-4fa5-52be-b64d-080c7d0e12da" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{t({t}^{2}-1)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397782} :answer-field/id #uuid "b539044b-f263-566b-a583-7f00bd13bba1" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397781}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "01283c40-bda7-59b4-8f16-b320c423d254" :question/math-academy-id "q-365387" :question/problem "Consider the d’Alembert differential equation\n\n$$\ny=x\\cdot \\frac{1}{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nUsing the substitution $t=\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ the equation can be transformed to a first-order linear equation of the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+P(t)x=Q(t).\n$$\n\nFind the functions $P$ and $Q.$\n\n$\\,\\,P(t)=$\n{{field-1}}$,\\,Q(t)=$\n{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "A differential equation is a d'Alembert differential equation if it can be expressed in the form\n\n$$\ny=xf({y}^{′})+g({y}^{′}),\n$$\n\nwhere $f$ and $g$ are differentiable functions of ${y}^{′}.$ \n\nIn our case, we wish to transform the equation\n\n$$\ny=x\\cdot \\frac{1}{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\n$$\n\ninto a first-order linear equation for $x=x(t).$ We proceed in three steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Substitute, and differentiate with respect to $x.$\n\nWe have the substitution\n\n$$\nt=\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the equation gives\n\n$$\ny=x\\cdot \\frac{1}{t}+t.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating both sides with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x\\cdot \\frac{1}{t}+t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the sum and product rules and using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x\\cdot \\frac{1}{t})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(t) \\\\ t & =\\frac{1}{t}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x)+x\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{t})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(t) \\\\ t & =\\frac{1}{t}-\\frac{x}{{t}^{2}}\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Isolate the derivative and take its reciprocal.\n\nWe isolate $\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t-\\frac{1}{t} & =-\\frac{x}{{t}^{2}}\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x} \\\\ \\frac{{t}^{2}-1}{t} & =(\\frac{{t}^{2}-x}{{t}^{2}})\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x} \\\\ \\frac{t({t}^{2}-1)}{{t}^{2}-x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{t({t}^{2}-1)}{{t}^{2}-x}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by taking the reciprocal of each side, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{{t}^{2}-x}{t({t}^{2}-1)}.\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Express the derivative as a first-order linear equation.\n\nFinally, we write this in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+P(t)x=Q(t)\n$$\n\nas follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{{t}^{2}-x}{t({t}^{2}-1)} \\\\ t({t}^{2}-1)\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & ={t}^{2}-x \\\\ t({t}^{2}-1)\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+x & ={t}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{x}{t({t}^{2}-1)} & =\\frac{{t}^{2}}{t({t}^{2}-1)} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{x}{t({t}^{2}-1)} & =\\frac{t}{{t}^{2}-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\nP(t)=\\frac{1}{t({t}^{2}-1)},\\,Q(t)=\\frac{t}{{t}^{2}-1}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115889 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7dcbd56f-0fc2-59c5-a016-4272be4734c7" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Points at Which a Function is Not Differentiable"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6f7c45a8-6d0f-5a1d-b8dc-4e8e1b4b6ce1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=2$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396668} {:answer/id #uuid "f7475fa2-2dfb-5ab6-af30-4b6beda786d6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-2$ and $x=2$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396669} {:answer/id #uuid "1541152a-7611-5c14-a588-d1e5cc5e22df" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-2$, $x=0$, and $x=2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396670} {:answer/id #uuid "453cb038-db89-5863-98ef-ba1dcdd04c12" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=0$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396671} {:answer/id #uuid "edc154f2-6797-5d5a-be28-0f6a1f59d668" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-2$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396672}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1541152a-7611-5c14-a588-d1e5cc5e22df" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-2$, $x=0$, and $x=2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396670} :answer-field/id #uuid "71e2164a-d23d-528c-85fa-147024e56737" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396667}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "097f3e11-2ce8-55f7-bbfa-1640ee282b68" :question/math-academy-id "q-47650" :question/problem "![](images/8e/8e7daf95fadbe2262a09e8183f00d63075655f3b97dd363984c192ac3414ad17.png)\n\nConsider the function $y=f(x)$ whose graph is plotted above. At which points is the function $f(x)$ **not** differentiable?" :question/worked-solution "The function is not differentiable wherever it has a discontinuity, corner, cusp, or vertical tangent.\n\n- At $x=-2$ the function has a cusp, so it's not differentiable there.\n\n- At $x=0$ the function has a jump discontinuity, so it's not differentiable there.\n\n- At $x=2$ the function has an infinite discontinuity, so it's not differentiable there.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $x=-2$, $x=0$, and $x=2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069374 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "31eacf5a-75a5-5b38-8c24-4b1598ecf3a7" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Points of Discontinuity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e9d17f27-11e4-5d07-bef6-23b79d1c374c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\pm 2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396674} {:answer/id #uuid "51dfca4a-207b-5033-87d0-8f1fd9a9fe6a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=2$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396675} {:answer/id #uuid "a59fc894-dd6b-5b7f-b8bc-d6156ee4de53" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=0$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396676} {:answer/id #uuid "fb712dc7-5f7d-5b94-ab66-e243d5ea0505" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=2$ and $x=0$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396677} {:answer/id #uuid "e25593d7-0095-53cf-b3dd-44c1e9a1bd39" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-2$ and $x=0$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396678}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e25593d7-0095-53cf-b3dd-44c1e9a1bd39" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-2$ and $x=0$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396678} :answer-field/id #uuid "17b83f56-ed58-5b0c-a67a-c211740c88f2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396673}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ea8e67f7-a331-5a5a-8338-679b56a16346" :question/math-academy-id "q-48080" :question/problem "![](images/37/374abcb470bfde0688def89290c8c1d639bf025afde150ccb9dd848c1fcc9a0f.png)\n\nWhat are the points of discontinuity of the function plotted above?" :question/worked-solution "The graph breaks at $x=-2$ and $x=0$ meaning that we can't draw the plot without taking our pencil off of the paper when passing through these points.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $x=-2$ and $x=0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186396679 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "31eacf5a-75a5-5b38-8c24-4b1598ecf3a7" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Points of Discontinuity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0f8cb089-6505-515d-b264-99e427a1221e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-3$ and $x=0$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396681} {:answer/id #uuid "c08b5c35-a5d7-5069-b56f-c620adb60e5b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=0$ and $x=2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396682} {:answer/id #uuid "86dd1c08-dfcc-5440-8ed6-a1599aed6c51" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\pm 2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396683} {:answer/id #uuid "c8a54622-a441-5f4f-8a9d-6f28f972e33c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-3$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396684} {:answer/id #uuid "50484ca8-86cd-5313-806f-14cb83956a21" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=1$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396685}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0f8cb089-6505-515d-b264-99e427a1221e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-3$ and $x=0$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396681} :answer-field/id #uuid "7d5e1e7b-8e38-5b41-953d-3eb5f39cc12b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396680}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f6e088c5-8b59-5061-af03-e9b40ee3d2a4" :question/math-academy-id "q-48088" :question/problem "![](images/e1/e111be8806fd5351b873219d9a790b71ca862a8fdef8b15df6477265cbb7ddea.png)\n\nWhat are the points of discontinuity of the function plotted above?" :question/worked-solution "The graph breaks at $x=-3$ and $x=0$ meaning that we can't draw the plot without taking our pencil off of the paper when passing through these points.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $x=-3$ and $x=0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186396686 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4f663b0b-9b5e-512c-92bb-0ea84d5baac5" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing the Limit of a Left Riemann Sum as a Definite Integral"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e499c456-6a1c-5152-933c-28134d048ffb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f(x)=2{e}^{x}$ and $b=\\operatorname{ln}⁡2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396688} {:answer/id #uuid "f947c798-bc89-571a-a666-d5ac5fe1b843" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f(x)={e}^{2x}$ and $b=\\operatorname{ln}⁡2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396689} {:answer/id #uuid "085d6ab0-e2f9-55ab-a353-c2e9ceb0a9dc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f(x)=2{e}^{x}$ and $b=2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396690} {:answer/id #uuid "6da93e3c-bfd8-566a-bf85-bf589185d302" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f(x)={e}^{x}$ and $b=\\operatorname{ln}⁡2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396691} {:answer/id #uuid "b72fbfe6-34a0-5341-a7d6-2590a001b91e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f(x)={e}^{x}$ and $b=2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396692}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e499c456-6a1c-5152-933c-28134d048ffb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f(x)=2{e}^{x}$ and $b=\\operatorname{ln}⁡2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396688} :answer-field/id #uuid "eb2a4470-ddb9-5a4f-aab9-d2401a7ffff8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396687}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "6a62fd18-f127-592a-8fe3-1cc5a47bb7fb" :question/math-academy-id "q-49234" :question/problem "Find the function $f(x)$ and the value of $b,$ given that ${\\int }_{0}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}[2{e}^{(k\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)/n}\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{n}].$" :question/worked-solution "A definite integral can be defined as the limit of a left Riemann sum as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{k})\\cdot Δx\n$$\n\nwhere $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}$ and ${x}_{k}=a+kΔx.$\n\nNotice that we're given that the lower limit $a=0.$ \n\nComparing the given summation with the general expression above, we see that $Δx=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{n}.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{n}=\\frac{b-0}{n}\\,⟹\\,b=\\operatorname{ln}⁡2.\n$$\n\nConsequently,  ${x}_{k}=\\frac{k\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{n}$ and $f({x}_{k})=2{e}^{{x}_{k}}.$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}2{e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}[2{e}^{(k\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)/n}\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{n}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $f(x)=2{e}^{x}$ and $b=\\operatorname{ln}⁡2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186396693 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "149fa184-850f-52d8-a309-4b79a371a74d" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Intervals on Which a Function is Invertible Given a Description"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2d49c863-bcca-5003-b60b-2402ac1bbc16" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396695} {:answer/id #uuid "372a1527-fcb7-5e97-9214-ceab892980ce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396696} {:answer/id #uuid "89d11ef2-0ebb-56ef-85f5-eff24a1c20ef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396697} {:answer/id #uuid "6e8bd46d-8040-58fd-9418-5e14e16fd9fd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396698} {:answer/id #uuid "3ecd9f23-0f52-529d-9d3d-4b581fbbb61a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396699}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6e8bd46d-8040-58fd-9418-5e14e16fd9fd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396698} :answer-field/id #uuid "fb55a4d3-ef9d-5ba1-bc8d-64b5b8502eb6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396694}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8061fba9-94fb-59ab-bb67-c981e34d25e7" :question/math-academy-id "q-66724" :question/problem "Given that $f(x)=2x+4,$ on which of the following intervals is $f(x)$ invertible?\n\nI. $x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )$\nII. $x\\in (10,\\infty )$\nIII. $x\\in [-5,5)$" :question/worked-solution "The function $f(x)$ is a linear function with slope $2$ and $y$-intercept $4,$ as shown below. \n\n![](images/d3/d33314230419ec762bc4ad8603b2e6c3436461cd6b4b6fe4783d12a23d01b660.png)\n\nA function is invertible on a particular interval if and only if it's a one-to-one function on that interval. To determine the intervals on which the function is invertible, we use the horizontal line test.\n\n- The given function is invertible on the interval $x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ On this interval, a horizontal line will intersect the curve at most once, no matter where we draw the line.\n\n- Since the function is invertible on $x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ it is also invertible on  $x\\in (10,\\infty )$ and $x\\in [-5,5).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186396700 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5130f54d-2a53-5373-81a6-355921ea7523" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Vector That Makes a Set Linearly Dependent"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "af7cf2f2-a407-5089-94fd-71e651ca7964" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ 20\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396702} {:answer/id #uuid "4693ebba-1176-550d-b873-45ee14bc37e2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-20 \\\\ -8\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396703} {:answer/id #uuid "5f1a7171-2230-5136-86ce-13f9058caed9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}20 \\\\ -10\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396704} {:answer/id #uuid "0a130a78-a08b-509f-a270-8fb414ea9872" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}20 \\\\ -8\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396705} {:answer/id #uuid "d231b096-e189-5829-b3fd-2b35888e10c4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-8 \\\\ 20\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396706}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0a130a78-a08b-509f-a270-8fb414ea9872" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}20 \\\\ -8\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396705} :answer-field/id #uuid "5c4ee9d3-9826-59fa-81e1-d30802c66a1e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396701}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2a792333-1cd7-596e-9f36-ea2d3aa5343e" :question/math-academy-id "q-75640" :question/problem "The set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ is linearly dependent. Which of the following vectors could be ${a}_{2}$ if\n${a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]?$" :question/worked-solution "We say that two vectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ are *linearly independent* if the only solution to the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{2}=0\n$$\n\nis ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}=0.$ Otherwise, the vectors are *linearly dependent*.\n\nA set containing *only two* vectors is linearly *dependent* if and only if the two vectors are parallel.\n\nFrom the given options, the correct answer is \n\n$$\n{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}20 \\\\ -8\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo see why, notice that \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}20 \\\\ -8\\end{aligned}]=4[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]=4{a}_{1}.\n$$\n\nThis way, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{a}_{1}-4{a}_{1} & =0 \\\\ 4{a}_{1}-{a}_{2} & =0 \\\\ 4{a}_{1}+(-1){a}_{2} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn other words, the vectors ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{2}$ are parallel.\n\nTo see why this means that ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{2}$ are linearly dependent, we notice that the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}=0\n$$\n\nhas the solution ${x}_{1}=4$ and ${x}_{2}=-1,$ which is different from ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}=0.$ Therefore, the set is linearly dependent."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186396707 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5130f54d-2a53-5373-81a6-355921ea7523" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Vector That Makes a Set Linearly Dependent"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fc07a05b-0118-5252-b35b-ce58aaf33f50" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-6 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396709} {:answer/id #uuid "013971a3-aab2-52a1-a19d-7a5ac0084ba8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396710} {:answer/id #uuid "142ba162-ec92-5e12-9e2e-f1802915a4bf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 6 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396711} {:answer/id #uuid "2d0e2276-3b1c-55bc-8e0d-1b1456cbdc62" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396712} {:answer/id #uuid "7d2d7aa3-f1f3-582e-b408-82340acf0b62" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396713}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2d0e2276-3b1c-55bc-8e0d-1b1456cbdc62" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396712} :answer-field/id #uuid "80b712db-bceb-5724-a93f-11876084e171" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396708}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1429f843-5ee1-5edb-b1a8-4da2570633ca" :question/math-academy-id "q-75645" :question/problem "The set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ is linearly dependent. Which of the following vectors could be ${a}_{2}$ if\n${a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]?$" :question/worked-solution "We say that two vectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ are *linearly independent* if the only solution to the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{2}=0\n$$\n\nis ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}=0.$ Otherwise, the vectors are *linearly dependent*.\n\nA set containing *only two* vectors is linearly *dependent* if and only if the two vectors are parallel.\n\nFrom the given options, the correct answer is\n\n$$\n{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo see why, notice that \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]=-2[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]=-2{a}_{1}.\n$$\n\nThis way, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{a}_{1}-2{a}_{1} & =0 \\\\ 2{a}_{1}+{a}_{2} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn other words, the vectors ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{2}$ are parallel.\n\nTo see why this means that ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{2}$ are linearly dependent, we notice that the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}=0\n$$\n\nhas the solution ${x}_{1}=2$ and ${x}_{2}=1,$ which is different from ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}=0.$ Therefore, the set is linearly dependent."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186396714 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cf99414e-f1cf-528f-adbb-407cf6e82c4c" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Linear Transformation Represented by a Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c162ec8c-668b-5bfc-ab23-8423cf76c91f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396716} {:answer/id #uuid "62baa2db-7c19-5634-b5e6-1d42635e4fb5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396717} {:answer/id #uuid "e8c0ccfc-1a58-56d8-bf17-b789f1ca213c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396718} {:answer/id #uuid "c8045e89-1656-55f6-a0dd-d7b1b98bab9a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396719} {:answer/id #uuid "d3f965ef-6b47-527f-a571-556003d4b579" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396720}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "62baa2db-7c19-5634-b5e6-1d42635e4fb5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396717} :answer-field/id #uuid "d53d07f6-e520-5ab9-8077-eda6c3ac37ba" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396715}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "1040f4bd-3d1a-5f7b-8b2b-4c581d4ad345" :question/math-academy-id "q-78297" :question/problem "Which of the following represents a reflection across the $x$-axis?" :question/worked-solution "The correct answer is $T=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}].$ Indeed, consider the standard basis ${i,j}$ of the Euclidean plane ${R}^{2}$ and the unit square spanned by the vectors, as shown below. \n\n![](images/9e/9e322e6ed1ddb8f1d094633e931cd179b7eb9d6f47acd7c7d0809f113df91b22.png)\n\nNow, we can find the images of ${i,j}$ under $T$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{i}^{′} & =T(i)=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=i \\\\ {j}^{′} & =T(j)=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]=-j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following image:\n\n![](images/b9/b93bb91dae85a3e3b40a8d0ade3d4f420064aaa6926431fa7f664c977bb8fd59.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186396721 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cf99414e-f1cf-528f-adbb-407cf6e82c4c" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Linear Transformation Represented by a Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "272ad4ce-b146-5a57-8c5a-5d1e1b3e79ba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396723} {:answer/id #uuid "b7fe5771-8880-5a10-a850-ea4baad6b72e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396724} {:answer/id #uuid "c2191dbe-147e-5390-a126-2d6a0061ee2a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396725} {:answer/id #uuid "c22c0f50-7b0a-5358-91f1-6489ddb8ffdd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396726} {:answer/id #uuid "ace98a54-10d8-5584-91a9-8dc23cfa3eb3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396727}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c22c0f50-7b0a-5358-91f1-6489ddb8ffdd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396726} :answer-field/id #uuid "47512c4c-4d28-5025-9b41-fddc19cb1574" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396722}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "a51c70cd-319b-5de1-a71f-816af37c3370" :question/math-academy-id "q-78302" :question/problem "Which of the following represents a reflection in the line $y=-x?$" :question/worked-solution "The correct answer is $T=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}].$ Indeed, consider the standard basis ${i,j}$ of the Euclidean plane ${R}^{2}$ and the unit square spanned by the vectors, as shown below. \n\n![](images/9e/9e322e6ed1ddb8f1d094633e931cd179b7eb9d6f47acd7c7d0809f113df91b22.png)\n\nNow, we can find the images of ${i,j}$ under $T$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{i}^{′} & =T(i)=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]=-j \\\\ {j}^{′} & =T(j)=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=-i\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following image:\n\n![](images/4a/4a83673a409fd2e5d33b0b5f743f57af08cfb27c38e312a636179102c2172b04.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186396728 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8d9b62ae-7545-5d65-bcf7-74ab0e8662ec" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Sets That Are Not Vector Spaces"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dced25c9-e7ab-5f35-8521-98dbb4380860" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "addition of polynomials is not associative" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396730} {:answer/id #uuid "44e5f623-8068-52a9-b610-cc58b0a625bb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "addition of polynomials is not commutative" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396731} {:answer/id #uuid "93ab4050-ad19-5d1c-8155-556dd79d719e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$1\\cdot f\\ne f$ for some $f\\in S$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396732} {:answer/id #uuid "4542450e-69bf-52e8-919b-58face6ea7bb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "multiplication of functions in  $S$ by real scalars is not closed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396733} {:answer/id #uuid "9ba8a7fb-33bd-5b6d-a7ee-19ce6a4656ed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$S$ does not contain the zero function" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396734}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4542450e-69bf-52e8-919b-58face6ea7bb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "multiplication of functions in  $S$ by real scalars is not closed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396733} :answer-field/id #uuid "689b4114-c1d3-5f0f-8182-74e6a0c999f0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396729}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f0cbcdef-8d32-524c-bf12-b31a8feacf80" :question/math-academy-id "q-94798" :question/problem "Let $S$ be the set of all polynomials in the real  variable $x$ with coefficients in $Q.$ Complete the following sentence: \n\n*$S$ is **not** a vector space (with respect to polynomials addition and multiplication of a polynomial by a real number) because $\\text{_________}\\,.$*" :question/worked-solution "The correct answer is as follows:\n\n*$S$ is **not** a vector space (with respect to polynomials addition and multiplication of a polynomial by a real number) because **multiplication of functions in  $S$ by real scalars is not closed.** *\n\nIndeed, let's consider, for example, the polynomial  $f(x)=x.$ Then $f\\in S$, but $\\sqrt{2}f(x)=\\sqrt{2}\\,\\text{ }x\\notin S$ because $\\sqrt{2}\\notin Q.$\nOn the other hand: \n\n- Addition of polynomials is associative. $\\,✓$\n\n- Addition of polynomials is commutative. $\\,✓$\n\n- The zero function belongs to $S.$ $\\,✓$\n\n- $1\\cdot f=f$ for all $f\\in S.$ $\\,✓$"}]}
